1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,513

2
00:00:05,513 --> 00:00:06,680
Thank you all for having me.

3
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:08,480
It's exciting to be here.

4
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:10,540
One of my favorite
things is talking about

5
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:13,240
what is going on
inside neural networks,

6
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:15,170
or at least what we're
trying to figure out

7
00:00:15,170 --> 00:00:16,628
is going on inside
neural networks.

8
00:00:16,628 --> 00:00:18,370
So it's always fun
to chat about that.

9
00:00:18,370 --> 00:00:20,920

10
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:23,350
Oh, gosh, I have to figure
out how to kind of do things.

11
00:00:23,350 --> 00:00:24,400
OK.

12
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,120
What?

13
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:26,620
OK.

14
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:27,700
There we go.

15
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:28,970
Now, we are advancing slides.

16
00:00:28,970 --> 00:00:30,680
That seems promising.

17
00:00:30,680 --> 00:00:33,730
So I think interpretability
means lots of different things

18
00:00:33,730 --> 00:00:35,560
to different people.

19
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:36,980
It's a very broad term.

20
00:00:36,980 --> 00:00:40,550
And people mean all sorts
of different things by it.

21
00:00:40,550 --> 00:00:42,970
And so I wanted to talk
just briefly about the kind

22
00:00:42,970 --> 00:00:45,160
of interpretability that
I spend my time thinking

23
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:48,520
about, which is what I'd call
mechanistic interpretability.

24
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:52,750
So most of my work actually
has not been on language models

25
00:00:52,750 --> 00:00:56,620
or on RNNs or transformers,
but on understanding vision

26
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:59,470
ConvNets and trying
to understand

27
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:01,870
how do the parameters
in those models

28
00:01:01,870 --> 00:01:04,720
actually map to algorithms.

29
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:06,970
So you can think of the
parameters of a neural network

30
00:01:06,970 --> 00:01:09,190
as being like a compiled
computer program

31
00:01:09,190 --> 00:01:12,820
and that the neurons are kind
of like variables or registers.

32
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:16,930
And somehow there are these
complex computer programs that

33
00:01:16,930 --> 00:01:18,640
are embedded in those weights.

34
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:21,220
And we'd like to turn them
back in to computer programs

35
00:01:21,220 --> 00:01:22,660
that humans can understand.

36
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:26,510
It's a kind of reverse
engineering problem.

37
00:01:26,510 --> 00:01:28,668
And so this is kind
of a fun example

38
00:01:28,668 --> 00:01:30,460
that we found where
there was a car neuron.

39
00:01:30,460 --> 00:01:34,360
And you could actually see
that we have the car neuron.

40
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:37,060
And it's constructed
from a wheel neuron.

41
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:38,308
And it looks for--

42
00:01:38,308 --> 00:01:39,850
in the case of the
wheel neuron, it's

43
00:01:39,850 --> 00:01:42,055
looking for the
wheels on the bottom.

44
00:01:42,055 --> 00:01:43,180
Those are positive weights.

45
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:44,780
And it doesn't want
to see them on top.

46
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:46,155
So it has negative
weights there.

47
00:01:46,155 --> 00:01:47,590
And there's also
a window neuron.

48
00:01:47,590 --> 00:01:50,390
It's looking for the windows on
the top and not on the bottom.

49
00:01:50,390 --> 00:01:52,057
And so what we're
actually seeing there,

50
00:01:52,057 --> 00:01:53,440
right, it's an algorithm.

51
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:56,170
It's an algorithm
that goes and turns.

52
00:01:56,170 --> 00:02:00,790
It's saying, well, cars
has wheels on the bottom

53
00:02:00,790 --> 00:02:03,040
and windows on the top
and chrome in the middle.

54
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:06,092
And that's actually just the
strongest neurons for that.

55
00:02:06,092 --> 00:02:08,259
And so we're actually seeing
a meaningful algorithm.

56
00:02:08,259 --> 00:02:10,030
And that's not an exception.

57
00:02:10,030 --> 00:02:12,580
That's sort of the general
story that if you're

58
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:14,707
willing to go and look at
neural network weights,

59
00:02:14,707 --> 00:02:16,540
and you're willing to
invest a lot of energy

60
00:02:16,540 --> 00:02:17,998
in trying to
reverse-engineer them,

61
00:02:17,998 --> 00:02:21,130
there's meaningful algorithms
written in the weights waiting

62
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:22,967
for you to find them.

63
00:02:22,967 --> 00:02:24,550
And there's a bunch
of reasons I think

64
00:02:24,550 --> 00:02:26,342
that's an interesting
thing to think about.

65
00:02:26,342 --> 00:02:28,843
One is just no one knows
how to go and do the things

66
00:02:28,843 --> 00:02:30,010
that neural networks can do.

67
00:02:30,010 --> 00:02:31,990
Like, no one knows how to
write a computer program that

68
00:02:31,990 --> 00:02:33,620
can accurately
classify ImageNet,

69
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:36,040
let alone the language modeling
tasks that we're doing.

70
00:02:36,040 --> 00:02:37,810
No one knows how
to directly write

71
00:02:37,810 --> 00:02:40,332
a computer program that can
do the things that GPT-3 does.

72
00:02:40,332 --> 00:02:42,790
And yet somehow gradient descent
is able to go and discover

73
00:02:42,790 --> 00:02:43,540
a way to do this.

74
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:45,160
And I want to know
what's going on.

75
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:48,700
I want to know what has
it discovered that it

76
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:51,572
can do in these systems?

77
00:02:51,572 --> 00:02:53,780
There's another reason why
I think this is important,

78
00:02:53,780 --> 00:02:56,200
which is safety.

79
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:59,290
So if we want to go and use
these systems in places where

80
00:02:59,290 --> 00:03:01,720
they have a big
effect on the world,

81
00:03:01,720 --> 00:03:03,100
I think a question
we need to ask

82
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:07,233
ourselves is, what happens
when these models have

83
00:03:07,233 --> 00:03:09,400
unanticipated failure modes,
failure modes we didn't

84
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:12,100
know how to go and test for or
to look for or to check for?

85
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:14,050
How can we discover
those things, especially

86
00:03:14,050 --> 00:03:15,925
if they're really
pathological failure modes,

87
00:03:15,925 --> 00:03:18,467
that the model's, in some sense,
deliberately doing something

88
00:03:18,467 --> 00:03:19,450
that we don't want?

89
00:03:19,450 --> 00:03:22,278
Well, the only way that I
really see that we can do that

90
00:03:22,278 --> 00:03:24,070
is if we can get to a
point where we really

91
00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:27,100
understand what's going
on inside these systems.

92
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:30,280
And so that's another reason
that I'm interested in this.

93
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:32,590
Now, actually doing
interpretability

94
00:03:32,590 --> 00:03:35,890
on language models and
transformers, it's new to me.

95
00:03:35,890 --> 00:03:37,780
Before this year, I
spent, like, eight years

96
00:03:37,780 --> 00:03:40,750
working on trying to reverse
engineer ConvNets, and vision

97
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:42,040
models.

98
00:03:42,040 --> 00:03:45,250
And so the idea is in
this talk are new things

99
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:47,800
that I've been thinking
about with my collaborators.

100
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:50,140
And we're still probably
a month or two out,

101
00:03:50,140 --> 00:03:52,127
maybe longer from
publishing them.

102
00:03:52,127 --> 00:03:53,710
And this is also the
first public talk

103
00:03:53,710 --> 00:03:55,180
that I've given on it.

104
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:58,580
So the things I'm going to talk
about, I think, honestly, it's

105
00:03:58,580 --> 00:04:00,125
still a little bit
confused for me

106
00:04:00,125 --> 00:04:01,750
and definitely are
going to be confused

107
00:04:01,750 --> 00:04:02,875
in my articulation of them.

108
00:04:02,875 --> 00:04:04,888
So if I say things
that are confusing,

109
00:04:04,888 --> 00:04:06,430
please feel free to
ask me questions.

110
00:04:06,430 --> 00:04:07,390
There might be
some points for me

111
00:04:07,390 --> 00:04:09,400
to go quickly because
there's a lot of content.

112
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:11,830
But definitely at the end, I
will be available for a while

113
00:04:11,830 --> 00:04:14,500
to chat about this stuff.

114
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:19,570
And, yeah, also I apologize
if I'm unfamiliar with Zoom

115
00:04:19,570 --> 00:04:21,610
and make mistakes.

116
00:04:21,610 --> 00:04:27,060
But, yeah, so with that
said, let's dive in.

117
00:04:27,060 --> 00:04:31,810
And so I wanted to
start with a mystery.

118
00:04:31,810 --> 00:04:34,330
Before we go and
try to actually dig

119
00:04:34,330 --> 00:04:37,060
into what's going on
inside these models,

120
00:04:37,060 --> 00:04:40,210
I want to motivate it by
a really strange piece

121
00:04:40,210 --> 00:04:43,930
of behavior that we discovered
and wanted to understand.

122
00:04:43,930 --> 00:04:46,680

123
00:04:46,680 --> 00:04:48,430
And by the way, I
should say all this work

124
00:04:48,430 --> 00:04:51,670
is done with my
colleagues in Anthropic,

125
00:04:51,670 --> 00:04:55,180
and especially my colleagues
Catherine and Nelson.

126
00:04:55,180 --> 00:04:57,040
OK, so on to the mystery.

127
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:00,070
I think probably the most
interesting and most exciting

128
00:05:00,070 --> 00:05:04,270
thing about transformers
is their ability

129
00:05:04,270 --> 00:05:06,760
to do in-context learning,
or sometimes people

130
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:08,560
will call it meta learning.

131
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:12,040
The GPT-3 paper goes
and describes things

132
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:14,653
as language models
are few-shot learners.

133
00:05:14,653 --> 00:05:16,570
There's lots of impressive
things about GPT-3.

134
00:05:16,570 --> 00:05:17,945
But they choose
to focus on that.

135
00:05:17,945 --> 00:05:20,710
And now everyone's talking
about prompt engineering.

136
00:05:20,710 --> 00:05:25,570
And Andrej Karpathy was joking
about how software 3.0 is

137
00:05:25,570 --> 00:05:26,960
designing the prompt.

138
00:05:26,960 --> 00:05:28,510
And so the ability
of language models

139
00:05:28,510 --> 00:05:31,810
of these large transformers
to respond to their context

140
00:05:31,810 --> 00:05:34,060
and learn from their context
and change their behavior

141
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:36,250
in response to their
context, it really

142
00:05:36,250 --> 00:05:38,380
seems like probably
the most surprising

143
00:05:38,380 --> 00:05:42,460
and striking and remarkable
thing about them.

144
00:05:42,460 --> 00:05:45,940
And some of my
colleagues previously

145
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:48,740
published a paper that has a
trick in it that I really love,

146
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:51,190
which is so we're all used to
looking at learning curves.

147
00:05:51,190 --> 00:05:52,065
You train your model.

148
00:05:52,065 --> 00:05:56,960
And as your model trains,
the loss goes down.

149
00:05:56,960 --> 00:05:58,710
Sometimes it's a little
bit discontinuous.

150
00:05:58,710 --> 00:06:00,443
But it goes down.

151
00:06:00,443 --> 00:06:02,610
Another thing that you can
do is you can go and take

152
00:06:02,610 --> 00:06:03,420
a fully trained model.

153
00:06:03,420 --> 00:06:04,440
And you can go and ask--

154
00:06:04,440 --> 00:06:07,470
as we go through the context,
as we go and we predict

155
00:06:07,470 --> 00:06:09,330
the first token and
then the second token

156
00:06:09,330 --> 00:06:10,935
and the third
token, we get better

157
00:06:10,935 --> 00:06:13,560
at predicting each token because
we have more information to go

158
00:06:13,560 --> 00:06:15,060
and predict it on.

159
00:06:15,060 --> 00:06:17,670
So the first token,
the loss should

160
00:06:17,670 --> 00:06:19,022
be the entropy of the unigrams.

161
00:06:19,022 --> 00:06:21,480
And then the next token should
be the entry of the bigrams.

162
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:22,590
Then it falls.

163
00:06:22,590 --> 00:06:24,360
But it keeps falling.

164
00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:26,200
And it keeps getting better.

165
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:29,670
And in some sense, that's
the model's ability

166
00:06:29,670 --> 00:06:33,450
to go and predict, to go
and do in-context learning.

167
00:06:33,450 --> 00:06:36,095
The ability to go
and predict, to be

168
00:06:36,095 --> 00:06:37,470
better at predicting
later tokens

169
00:06:37,470 --> 00:06:40,140
than you are at predicting early
tokens, that is, in some sense,

170
00:06:40,140 --> 00:06:41,580
a mathematical
definition of what

171
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:44,400
it means to be good at this
magical in-context learning,

172
00:06:44,400 --> 00:06:47,030
or meta learning, that
these models can do.

173
00:06:47,030 --> 00:06:49,590
And so that's kind of cool
because that gives us a way

174
00:06:49,590 --> 00:06:51,420
to go and look at what
our models are good,

175
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:53,901
at in-context learning.

176
00:06:53,901 --> 00:06:54,570
Chris?

177
00:06:54,570 --> 00:06:54,840
Yeah.

178
00:06:54,840 --> 00:06:56,460
If I could just ask a
question, like a clarification

179
00:06:56,460 --> 00:06:56,875
on what you mean by that.

180
00:06:56,875 --> 00:06:57,900
Yeah, please.

181
00:06:57,900 --> 00:07:00,400
When you say learning, there
are no actual parameter updates

182
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:01,650
happening here, is that right?

183
00:07:01,650 --> 00:07:03,390
Oh, yeah, that is
the remarkable thing

184
00:07:03,390 --> 00:07:04,765
about in-context
learning, right?

185
00:07:04,765 --> 00:07:07,260
So, yeah, we traditionally
think about neural networks

186
00:07:07,260 --> 00:07:09,043
as learning over the
course of training

187
00:07:09,043 --> 00:07:10,710
by going and modifying
their parameters.

188
00:07:10,710 --> 00:07:12,960
But somehow models
appear to also be

189
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:14,573
able to learn, in some sense.

190
00:07:14,573 --> 00:07:16,740
If you give them a couple
examples in their context,

191
00:07:16,740 --> 00:07:18,907
they can then go and do
that later in their context,

192
00:07:18,907 --> 00:07:20,470
even though no
parameter's changed.

193
00:07:20,470 --> 00:07:23,970
And so it's some kind of quite
different notion of learning,

194
00:07:23,970 --> 00:07:27,505
as you're gesturing.

195
00:07:27,505 --> 00:07:28,880
I think that's
making more sense.

196
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:32,070
So I mean, could you also just
describe in-context learning

197
00:07:32,070 --> 00:07:34,980
in this case as conditioning,
as in, like, conditioning

198
00:07:34,980 --> 00:07:38,050
on the first 5 tokens
of a 10-token sentence?

199
00:07:38,050 --> 00:07:39,300
And predict the next 5 tokens?

200
00:07:39,300 --> 00:07:39,630
Yeah.

201
00:07:39,630 --> 00:07:40,920
I think the reason
that people sometimes

202
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:43,337
think about this as in-context
learning, or meta learning,

203
00:07:43,337 --> 00:07:46,510
is that you can do things where
you actually take a training

204
00:07:46,510 --> 00:07:47,010
set.

205
00:07:47,010 --> 00:07:48,968
And you embed the training
set in your context,

206
00:07:48,968 --> 00:07:50,700
like just two or three examples.

207
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:53,958
And then suddenly your model
can go and do this task.

208
00:07:53,958 --> 00:07:55,500
And so you can do
a few-shot learning

209
00:07:55,500 --> 00:07:58,030
by embedding things
in the context.

210
00:07:58,030 --> 00:07:58,530
[INAUDIBLE]

211
00:07:58,530 --> 00:08:00,405
So yeah, the formal
setup is that you're just

212
00:08:00,405 --> 00:08:02,920
conditioning on this context.

213
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:06,000
And it's just that
somehow this ability--

214
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:10,080
like there's some sense for
a long time, people were--

215
00:08:10,080 --> 00:08:12,240
I mean, I guess really
the history of this

216
00:08:12,240 --> 00:08:15,690
is that we started to get good
at neural networks learning,

217
00:08:15,690 --> 00:08:16,350
right?

218
00:08:16,350 --> 00:08:18,840
And we could go and train
language-- or train vision

219
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:19,830
models and language
models that could

220
00:08:19,830 --> 00:08:20,880
do all these remarkable things.

221
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:22,450
But then people
started to be like,

222
00:08:22,450 --> 00:08:25,530
well, these systems, they
take so many more examples

223
00:08:25,530 --> 00:08:26,970
than humans do to go and learn.

224
00:08:26,970 --> 00:08:28,320
How can we go and fix this?

225
00:08:28,320 --> 00:08:30,695
And we had all these ideas
about meta learning developed,

226
00:08:30,695 --> 00:08:33,943
where we want it to go and
train models explicitly

227
00:08:33,943 --> 00:08:35,610
to be able to learn
from a few examples.

228
00:08:35,610 --> 00:08:37,735
And people developed all
these complicated schemes.

229
00:08:37,735 --> 00:08:40,740
And the truly, absurd thing
about transformer language

230
00:08:40,740 --> 00:08:43,049
models is without
any effort at all,

231
00:08:43,049 --> 00:08:45,220
we get this for free,
that you can go and just

232
00:08:45,220 --> 00:08:47,220
give them a couple of
examples in their context.

233
00:08:47,220 --> 00:08:48,750
And they can learn
in their context

234
00:08:48,750 --> 00:08:50,830
to go and do new things.

235
00:08:50,830 --> 00:08:51,750
I think that was--

236
00:08:51,750 --> 00:08:54,060
that was, in some sense,
the most striking thing

237
00:08:54,060 --> 00:08:56,500
about the GPT-3 paper.

238
00:08:56,500 --> 00:09:00,780
And so yeah, this
ability to go and have

239
00:09:00,780 --> 00:09:02,520
the just conditioning
on a context

240
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:05,970
go and give you new abilities
for free and the ability

241
00:09:05,970 --> 00:09:09,128
to generalize to new
things is, in some sense,

242
00:09:09,128 --> 00:09:10,920
to me, the most striking
and shocking thing

243
00:09:10,920 --> 00:09:14,800
about transformer
language models.

244
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:15,530
It makes sense.

245
00:09:15,530 --> 00:09:19,930
I guess from my
perspective, I'm trying

246
00:09:19,930 --> 00:09:24,430
to square the notion of learning
in this case with if you

247
00:09:24,430 --> 00:09:28,060
or I were given a prompt of,
like, 1 plus 1 equals 2, 2

248
00:09:28,060 --> 00:09:32,410
plus 3 equals 5 as the
sort of few-shot setup.

249
00:09:32,410 --> 00:09:36,310
And then somebody else
put, like, 5 plus 3 equals,

250
00:09:36,310 --> 00:09:37,450
and we had to fill it out.

251
00:09:37,450 --> 00:09:40,150
In that case I wouldn't say
that we've learned arithmetic

252
00:09:40,150 --> 00:09:41,830
because we already
sort of knew it.

253
00:09:41,830 --> 00:09:44,170
But rather we're just sort
of conditioning on the prompt

254
00:09:44,170 --> 00:09:48,150
to know what it is that we
should then generate, right?

255
00:09:48,150 --> 00:09:49,825
But it seems to me like that's--

256
00:09:49,825 --> 00:09:50,325
So that's--

257
00:09:50,325 --> 00:09:50,918
Uh-huh?

258
00:09:50,918 --> 00:09:52,460
Yeah, I think that's
on the spectrum,

259
00:09:52,460 --> 00:09:54,490
though, because you
can also go and give

260
00:09:54,490 --> 00:09:57,850
completely nonsensical problems,
where the model would never

261
00:09:57,850 --> 00:09:59,893
have seen, like,
mimic this function

262
00:09:59,893 --> 00:10:01,810
and give a couple of
examples of the function.

263
00:10:01,810 --> 00:10:03,352
And the model's
never seen it before.

264
00:10:03,352 --> 00:10:05,680
And it can go and do that
later in the context.

265
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:09,850
And I think what you did learn
in a lot of these cases--

266
00:10:09,850 --> 00:10:12,382
so you might not have
learned arithmetic.

267
00:10:12,382 --> 00:10:14,590
You might have had some
innate faculty for arithmetic

268
00:10:14,590 --> 00:10:15,340
that you're using.

269
00:10:15,340 --> 00:10:17,470
But you might have learned,
oh, OK, right now we're

270
00:10:17,470 --> 00:10:20,060
doing arithmetic problems.

271
00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:20,560
Got it.

272
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:23,143
And in the sense, I agree that
there's an element of semantics

273
00:10:23,143 --> 00:10:23,980
here.

274
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:24,640
Yeah.

275
00:10:24,640 --> 00:10:26,390
No, this is helpful
though just to clarify

276
00:10:26,390 --> 00:10:29,140
exactly sort of what you
mean by in-context learning.

277
00:10:29,140 --> 00:10:30,610
Thank you for
walking through this.

278
00:10:30,610 --> 00:10:33,420
Of course.

279
00:10:33,420 --> 00:10:37,160
So something that's I think
really striking about all of us

280
00:10:37,160 --> 00:10:39,020
is-- well, OK, so
we've talked about how

281
00:10:39,020 --> 00:10:41,390
we can sort of look
at the learning curve,

282
00:10:41,390 --> 00:10:43,760
and we can also look at this
in-context learning curve.

283
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:45,740
But really, those
are just two slices

284
00:10:45,740 --> 00:10:47,970
of a two-dimensional space.

285
00:10:47,970 --> 00:10:50,570
So in some sense, the
more fundamental thing

286
00:10:50,570 --> 00:10:53,540
is how good are we at predicting
the n-th token at any given

287
00:10:53,540 --> 00:10:54,980
point in training?

288
00:10:54,980 --> 00:10:58,268
And something that you'll
notice if you look at this--

289
00:10:58,268 --> 00:10:59,810
so when we talk
about the loss curve,

290
00:10:59,810 --> 00:11:02,810
where we're just talking if you
average over this dimension,

291
00:11:02,810 --> 00:11:05,510
if you average like
this and project

292
00:11:05,510 --> 00:11:09,140
on to the training step,
that's your loss curve.

293
00:11:09,140 --> 00:11:12,350
And then the thing that we are
calling in-context learning

294
00:11:12,350 --> 00:11:15,560
curve is just this line--

295
00:11:15,560 --> 00:11:18,740
yeah, this line down
at the end here.

296
00:11:18,740 --> 00:11:21,580

297
00:11:21,580 --> 00:11:23,330
And something that's
kind of striking

298
00:11:23,330 --> 00:11:26,720
is there's this
discontinuity in it.

299
00:11:26,720 --> 00:11:29,720
There's this point
where the model seems

300
00:11:29,720 --> 00:11:33,200
to get radically better in
a very, very short time span

301
00:11:33,200 --> 00:11:35,580
and going and
predicting late tokens.

302
00:11:35,580 --> 00:11:37,760
So it's not that different
in early time steps.

303
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:40,010
But in late time steps
suddenly, you get better.

304
00:11:40,010 --> 00:11:43,650

305
00:11:43,650 --> 00:11:45,690
And a way that you can
make this more striking

306
00:11:45,690 --> 00:11:48,480
is you can take the
difference in your ability

307
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:51,135
to predict the 50th
token and your ability

308
00:11:51,135 --> 00:11:52,260
to predict the 500th token.

309
00:11:52,260 --> 00:11:56,790
You can subtract from the 500th
token the 50th token loss.

310
00:11:56,790 --> 00:12:02,212
And what you see is that
over the course of training,

311
00:12:02,212 --> 00:12:03,420
you're not very good at this.

312
00:12:03,420 --> 00:12:04,753
And you get a little bit better.

313
00:12:04,753 --> 00:12:06,720
And then suddenly,
you have this cliff.

314
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:08,928
And then you never get
better than-- the difference

315
00:12:08,928 --> 00:12:10,720
between these, at least,
never gets better.

316
00:12:10,720 --> 00:12:12,637
So the model gets better
at predicting things.

317
00:12:12,637 --> 00:12:14,100
But its ability
to go and predict

318
00:12:14,100 --> 00:12:17,770
late tokens over early
tokens never gets better.

319
00:12:17,770 --> 00:12:20,610
And so there's-- in the span
of just a few hundred steps

320
00:12:20,610 --> 00:12:23,850
in training, the model has
gotten radically better at its

321
00:12:23,850 --> 00:12:28,890
ability to go and do this
kind of in-context learning.

322
00:12:28,890 --> 00:12:32,170
And so you might ask, what's
going on at that point?

323
00:12:32,170 --> 00:12:33,773
And this is just one model.

324
00:12:33,773 --> 00:12:35,190
Well, so, first
of all, it's worth

325
00:12:35,190 --> 00:12:36,945
noting this isn't
a small change.

326
00:12:36,945 --> 00:12:40,680

327
00:12:40,680 --> 00:12:42,270
We don't think about
this very often.

328
00:12:42,270 --> 00:12:43,330
But often, we just
look at loss curves.

329
00:12:43,330 --> 00:12:45,090
And we're like, did the model
do better than another model

330
00:12:45,090 --> 00:12:46,830
or worse than another model?

331
00:12:46,830 --> 00:12:50,430
But you can think of
this in terms of nats.

332
00:12:50,430 --> 00:12:53,100
It's just the information
theoretic quantity.

333
00:12:53,100 --> 00:12:55,230
And you can convert
that into to bits.

334
00:12:55,230 --> 00:12:58,620
And so one way you can interpret
this is it's something roughly

335
00:12:58,620 --> 00:13:02,400
like the model at 0.4
nats is about 0.5 bits

336
00:13:02,400 --> 00:13:05,430
is about every other token the
model gets to go and sample

337
00:13:05,430 --> 00:13:07,365
twice and pick the better one.

338
00:13:07,365 --> 00:13:09,240
It's actually-- it's
even stronger than that.

339
00:13:09,240 --> 00:13:10,615
That's a sort of
an underestimate

340
00:13:10,615 --> 00:13:13,790
of how big a deal going and
getting better by 0.4 nats is.

341
00:13:13,790 --> 00:13:16,980
So this is a real big difference
in the model's ability

342
00:13:16,980 --> 00:13:20,697
to go and predict late tokens.

343
00:13:20,697 --> 00:13:22,530
And we can visualize
this in different ways.

344
00:13:22,530 --> 00:13:24,630
We can also go and ask,
how much better are we

345
00:13:24,630 --> 00:13:26,880
getting at going and
predicting later tokens?

346
00:13:26,880 --> 00:13:28,980
And look at the
derivative, and then we

347
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:30,540
can see very
clearly that there's

348
00:13:30,540 --> 00:13:33,390
some kind of discontinuity in
that derivative at this point.

349
00:13:33,390 --> 00:13:35,430
And we could take the
second derivative then.

350
00:13:35,430 --> 00:13:39,060
And we can-- well, derivative
with respect to training.

351
00:13:39,060 --> 00:13:42,510
And now we see that
there's very, very clearly

352
00:13:42,510 --> 00:13:43,690
this line here.

353
00:13:43,690 --> 00:13:47,160
So something in just the
span of a few hundred steps

354
00:13:47,160 --> 00:13:49,210
is causing some big change.

355
00:13:49,210 --> 00:13:52,170
And we have some kind of
phase change going on.

356
00:13:52,170 --> 00:13:55,620
And this is true
across model sizes.

357
00:13:55,620 --> 00:13:58,050
You can actually see it a
little bit in the loss curve.

358
00:13:58,050 --> 00:13:59,560
And there's this
little bump here.

359
00:13:59,560 --> 00:14:01,050
And that corresponds
to the point

360
00:14:01,050 --> 00:14:02,653
where you have this change.

361
00:14:02,653 --> 00:14:05,070
We actually could have seen
in the loss curve earlier too.

362
00:14:05,070 --> 00:14:08,760
It's this bump here.

363
00:14:08,760 --> 00:14:09,690
Excuse me.

364
00:14:09,690 --> 00:14:11,698
So we have this phase
change going on.

365
00:14:11,698 --> 00:14:13,990
And there's, I think, a really
tempting theory to have,

366
00:14:13,990 --> 00:14:18,420
which is that somehow,
this change in the model's

367
00:14:18,420 --> 00:14:21,270
output and its behaviors
and in these sort

368
00:14:21,270 --> 00:14:24,675
of outward-facing properties
corresponds presumably

369
00:14:24,675 --> 00:14:26,550
to some kind of change
in the algorithms that

370
00:14:26,550 --> 00:14:27,940
are running inside the model.

371
00:14:27,940 --> 00:14:30,420
So if we observe this big
phase change, especially

372
00:14:30,420 --> 00:14:33,783
in a very small window
in the model's behavior,

373
00:14:33,783 --> 00:14:35,700
presumably there's some
change in the circuits

374
00:14:35,700 --> 00:14:38,530
inside the model
that is driving it.

375
00:14:38,530 --> 00:14:40,240
At least, that's a
natural hypothesis.

376
00:14:40,240 --> 00:14:42,790
So if we want to
ask that, though, we

377
00:14:42,790 --> 00:14:44,260
need to go be able
to understand,

378
00:14:44,260 --> 00:14:46,270
what are the algorithms that's
running inside the model?

379
00:14:46,270 --> 00:14:48,095
How can we turn the
parameters in the model

380
00:14:48,095 --> 00:14:49,220
back into those algorithms?

381
00:14:49,220 --> 00:14:51,520
So that's going to be our goal.

382
00:14:51,520 --> 00:14:54,910
Now, it's going to require
us to cover a lot of ground

383
00:14:54,910 --> 00:14:56,615
in a relatively
short amount of time.

384
00:14:56,615 --> 00:14:58,240
So I'm going to go
a little bit quickly

385
00:14:58,240 --> 00:14:59,290
through the next section.

386
00:14:59,290 --> 00:15:02,080
And I will highlight sort
of the key takeaways.

387
00:15:02,080 --> 00:15:07,240
And then I will be very happy
to go and explore any of this

388
00:15:07,240 --> 00:15:08,170
in as much depth--

389
00:15:08,170 --> 00:15:10,690
I'm free for another
hour after this call

390
00:15:10,690 --> 00:15:12,520
and just happy to
talk in as much depth

391
00:15:12,520 --> 00:15:14,830
as people want about
the details of this.

392
00:15:14,830 --> 00:15:17,410
So it turns out
this phase change

393
00:15:17,410 --> 00:15:20,410
doesn't happen in a one-layer
attention-only transformer.

394
00:15:20,410 --> 00:15:22,910
And it does happen in a
two-layer attention-only

395
00:15:22,910 --> 00:15:23,410
transformer.

396
00:15:23,410 --> 00:15:25,577
So if we could understand
a one-layer attention-only

397
00:15:25,577 --> 00:15:28,180
transformer and a two-layer
attention-only transformer,

398
00:15:28,180 --> 00:15:33,170
that might give us a pretty
big clue as to what's going on.

399
00:15:33,170 --> 00:15:35,567
And so we're attention only.

400
00:15:35,567 --> 00:15:37,650
We're also going to leave
out LayerNorm and biases

401
00:15:37,650 --> 00:15:39,210
to simplify things.

402
00:15:39,210 --> 00:15:43,050
So one way you could describe
an attention-only transformer

403
00:15:43,050 --> 00:15:45,337
is we're going to
embed our tokens.

404
00:15:45,337 --> 00:15:47,670
And then we're going to apply
a bunch of attention heads

405
00:15:47,670 --> 00:15:49,528
and add them into
the residual strain

406
00:15:49,528 --> 00:15:50,820
and then apply our unembedding.

407
00:15:50,820 --> 00:15:52,790
And that will give
us our logits.

408
00:15:52,790 --> 00:15:55,290
And we could go and write that
out as equations, if we want,

409
00:15:55,290 --> 00:15:59,220
multiply it by an embedding
matrix, apply attention heads,

410
00:15:59,220 --> 00:16:03,400
and then compute the
logits behind embedding.

411
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:06,730
And the part here
that's a little tricky

412
00:16:06,730 --> 00:16:08,350
is understanding
the attention heads.

413
00:16:08,350 --> 00:16:10,460
And this might be a
somewhat conventional way

414
00:16:10,460 --> 00:16:11,710
of describing attention heads.

415
00:16:11,710 --> 00:16:14,290
And it actually kind
of obscures a lot

416
00:16:14,290 --> 00:16:15,850
of the structure
of attention heads.

417
00:16:15,850 --> 00:16:19,060
I think that oftentimes it's
we make attention heads more

418
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:20,560
complex than they
are, or we sort of

419
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:22,430
hide the interesting structure.

420
00:16:22,430 --> 00:16:23,470
So what is this saying?

421
00:16:23,470 --> 00:16:27,182
Well, it's saying, for every
token, compute a value vector.

422
00:16:27,182 --> 00:16:29,140
And then go and mix the
value vectors according

423
00:16:29,140 --> 00:16:30,280
to the attention matrix.

424
00:16:30,280 --> 00:16:32,590
And then project them
with the output matrix

425
00:16:32,590 --> 00:16:35,410
back into the residual string.

426
00:16:35,410 --> 00:16:38,380
So there's another
notation, which

427
00:16:38,380 --> 00:16:40,630
you could think of this
as using tensor products

428
00:16:40,630 --> 00:16:45,600
or using I guess left and
right multiplying this.

429
00:16:45,600 --> 00:16:47,350
There's a few ways you
can interpret this.

430
00:16:47,350 --> 00:16:50,650
But I'll just sort
of try to explain

431
00:16:50,650 --> 00:16:53,260
what this notation means.

432
00:16:53,260 --> 00:16:57,370
So this means for every
x, our residual stream,

433
00:16:57,370 --> 00:17:00,520
we have a vector for
every single token.

434
00:17:00,520 --> 00:17:04,270
And this means, go and multiply
independently the vector

435
00:17:04,270 --> 00:17:05,560
for each token by WV.

436
00:17:05,560 --> 00:17:09,400
So compute the value
vector for every token.

437
00:17:09,400 --> 00:17:10,900
This one, on the
other hand, means--

438
00:17:10,900 --> 00:17:12,260
notice that it's now on the--

439
00:17:12,260 --> 00:17:13,450
A is on the left-hand side.

440
00:17:13,450 --> 00:17:18,579
It means, go and multiply
the attention matrix--

441
00:17:18,579 --> 00:17:21,430
or go and do linear
combinations of value vectors.

442
00:17:21,430 --> 00:17:23,608
So don't change the
value vectors pointwise,

443
00:17:23,608 --> 00:17:25,900
but go and mix them together
according to the attention

444
00:17:25,900 --> 00:17:26,400
pattern.

445
00:17:26,400 --> 00:17:28,240
Create our weighted sum.

446
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:30,880
And then, again, independently
for every position,

447
00:17:30,880 --> 00:17:33,363
go and apply the output matrix.

448
00:17:33,363 --> 00:17:35,530
And you can apply the
distributive property to this.

449
00:17:35,530 --> 00:17:37,330
And it just reveals
that actually, it

450
00:17:37,330 --> 00:17:38,650
didn't matter that
you did the attention

451
00:17:38,650 --> 00:17:39,520
sort of in the middle.

452
00:17:39,520 --> 00:17:40,910
You could have done the
attention at the beginning.

453
00:17:40,910 --> 00:17:42,440
You could have
done it at the end.

454
00:17:42,440 --> 00:17:44,032
That's independent.

455
00:17:44,032 --> 00:17:45,490
And the thing that
actually matters

456
00:17:45,490 --> 00:17:48,820
is there's this WV WO
matrix that describes--

457
00:17:48,820 --> 00:17:50,342
what it's really saying is--

458
00:17:50,342 --> 00:17:54,102
WV WO describes what information
the attention head reads

459
00:17:54,102 --> 00:17:56,560
from each position and how it
writes it to its destination,

460
00:17:56,560 --> 00:18:01,390
whereas A describes which tokens
we read from and write to.

461
00:18:01,390 --> 00:18:03,940
And that's kind of getting
more the fundamental structure

462
00:18:03,940 --> 00:18:04,815
in an attention head.

463
00:18:04,815 --> 00:18:06,670
An attention head goes
and moves information

464
00:18:06,670 --> 00:18:08,110
from one position to another.

465
00:18:08,110 --> 00:18:11,170
And the process of which
position gets moved from

466
00:18:11,170 --> 00:18:13,915
and to is independent from
what information gets moved.

467
00:18:13,915 --> 00:18:17,360

468
00:18:17,360 --> 00:18:20,480
And if you rewrite your
transformer that way--

469
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:23,180
well, first, we can
go and write the sum

470
00:18:23,180 --> 00:18:25,160
of attention heads, just as--

471
00:18:25,160 --> 00:18:26,870
in this form.

472
00:18:26,870 --> 00:18:30,680
And then we can go in and
write that as the entire layer

473
00:18:30,680 --> 00:18:34,410
by going and adding
in an identity.

474
00:18:34,410 --> 00:18:37,760
And if we go and plug that
all in to our transformer

475
00:18:37,760 --> 00:18:43,980
and go and expand, we have
to go and multiply everything

476
00:18:43,980 --> 00:18:44,480
through.

477
00:18:44,480 --> 00:18:47,063
We get this
interesting equation.

478
00:18:47,063 --> 00:18:48,230
And so we get this one term.

479
00:18:48,230 --> 00:18:49,980
This corresponds to
just the path directly

480
00:18:49,980 --> 00:18:51,290
through the residual string.

481
00:18:51,290 --> 00:18:54,230
And it's going to want to
store bigram statistics.

482
00:18:54,230 --> 00:18:56,390
It's just-- all it gets
is the previous token

483
00:18:56,390 --> 00:18:58,190
and tries to predict
the next token.

484
00:18:58,190 --> 00:19:00,320
And so it gets to
try and predict--

485
00:19:00,320 --> 00:19:01,730
try to store bigram statistics.

486
00:19:01,730 --> 00:19:03,470
And then for every
attention head,

487
00:19:03,470 --> 00:19:05,225
we get this matrix
that says, OK, well, we

488
00:19:05,225 --> 00:19:06,350
have the attention pattern.

489
00:19:06,350 --> 00:19:07,970
So it looks-- that
describes which

490
00:19:07,970 --> 00:19:09,200
token looks at which token.

491
00:19:09,200 --> 00:19:10,790
And we have this
matrix here, which

492
00:19:10,790 --> 00:19:12,740
describes how, for
every possible token

493
00:19:12,740 --> 00:19:15,695
you could attend to, how
it affects the logits.

494
00:19:15,695 --> 00:19:17,570
And that's just a table
that you can look at.

495
00:19:17,570 --> 00:19:20,258
It just says, for this attention
head, it looks at this token.

496
00:19:20,258 --> 00:19:22,550
It's going to increase the
probability of these tokens.

497
00:19:22,550 --> 00:19:24,050
In a one-layer
attention-only transformer,

498
00:19:24,050 --> 00:19:25,070
that's all there is.

499
00:19:25,070 --> 00:19:29,070

500
00:19:29,070 --> 00:19:31,530
Yeah, so this is just
the interpretation

501
00:19:31,530 --> 00:19:34,680
I was describing.

502
00:19:34,680 --> 00:19:38,340
And another thing that's worth
noting is according to this,

503
00:19:38,340 --> 00:19:40,560
the attention-only
transformer is linear

504
00:19:40,560 --> 00:19:42,707
if you fix the
attention pattern.

505
00:19:42,707 --> 00:19:44,790
Now, of course, the attention
pattern isn't fixed.

506
00:19:44,790 --> 00:19:46,140
But whenever you
have the opportunity

507
00:19:46,140 --> 00:19:48,210
to go and make something
linear, linear functions

508
00:19:48,210 --> 00:19:49,550
are really easy to understand.

509
00:19:49,550 --> 00:19:51,240
And so if you can fix a
small number of things

510
00:19:51,240 --> 00:19:52,615
and make something
linear, that's

511
00:19:52,615 --> 00:19:54,970
actually a lot of leverage.

512
00:19:54,970 --> 00:19:57,640
OK.

513
00:19:57,640 --> 00:19:59,650
And yeah, we could talk
about how the attention

514
00:19:59,650 --> 00:20:02,290
pattern is computed as well.

515
00:20:02,290 --> 00:20:05,660
If you expand it out, you'll
get an equation like this.

516
00:20:05,660 --> 00:20:09,810
And notice-- well, I
think it'll be easier.

517
00:20:09,810 --> 00:20:10,310
OK.

518
00:20:10,310 --> 00:20:12,757

519
00:20:12,757 --> 00:20:14,590
I think the core story,
though, to take away

520
00:20:14,590 --> 00:20:17,220
from all of these is we
have these two matrices that

521
00:20:17,220 --> 00:20:18,470
actually look kind of similar.

522
00:20:18,470 --> 00:20:22,840
So this one here tells you,
if you attend to a token,

523
00:20:22,840 --> 00:20:24,580
how are the logits affected?

524
00:20:24,580 --> 00:20:27,070
And you can just think of
it as a giant matrix of,

525
00:20:27,070 --> 00:20:29,740
for every possible
input token, how

526
00:20:29,740 --> 00:20:32,720
is the logit-- how are the
logits affected by that token?

527
00:20:32,720 --> 00:20:34,692
Are they made more
likely or less likely?

528
00:20:34,692 --> 00:20:36,400
And we have this one,
which sort of says,

529
00:20:36,400 --> 00:20:39,460
how much does every token want
to attend to every other token?

530
00:20:39,460 --> 00:20:43,020

531
00:20:43,020 --> 00:20:44,880
One way that you
can picture this

532
00:20:44,880 --> 00:20:49,017
is, OK, there's really
three tokens involved

533
00:20:49,017 --> 00:20:50,850
when we're thinking
about an attention head.

534
00:20:50,850 --> 00:20:56,070
We have the token that we're
going to move information to

535
00:20:56,070 --> 00:20:57,990
and that's attending backwards.

536
00:20:57,990 --> 00:21:01,380
We have the source token that's
going to get attended to.

537
00:21:01,380 --> 00:21:03,090
And we have the output
token whose logits

538
00:21:03,090 --> 00:21:05,120
are going to be affected.

539
00:21:05,120 --> 00:21:06,830
And you can just
trace through this.

540
00:21:06,830 --> 00:21:08,660
So you can ask, what happens--

541
00:21:08,660 --> 00:21:12,150
how does attending to this
token affect the output?

542
00:21:12,150 --> 00:21:14,880
Well, first, we embed the token.

543
00:21:14,880 --> 00:21:18,500
Then we multiply by WV
to get the value vector.

544
00:21:18,500 --> 00:21:21,080
The information gets moved
by the attention pattern.

545
00:21:21,080 --> 00:21:23,570
We multiply by WO to add it
back into the residual string.

546
00:21:23,570 --> 00:21:25,040
We get hit by the end embedding.

547
00:21:25,040 --> 00:21:26,390
And we affect the logits.

548
00:21:26,390 --> 00:21:28,350
And that's where that
one matrix comes from.

549
00:21:28,350 --> 00:21:30,620
And we can also
ask, what decides

550
00:21:30,620 --> 00:21:33,620
whether a token gets a high
score when we're computing

551
00:21:33,620 --> 00:21:34,610
the attention pattern?

552
00:21:34,610 --> 00:21:39,360
And it just says,
embed the token.

553
00:21:39,360 --> 00:21:40,380
Turn it into a query.

554
00:21:40,380 --> 00:21:41,790
Embed the other token.

555
00:21:41,790 --> 00:21:43,320
Turn it into a key.

556
00:21:43,320 --> 00:21:44,220
And dot product them.

557
00:21:44,220 --> 00:21:47,980
And so that's where those
two matrices come from.

558
00:21:47,980 --> 00:21:52,080
So I know that I'm
going quite quickly.

559
00:21:52,080 --> 00:21:54,420
Maybe I'll just
briefly pause here.

560
00:21:54,420 --> 00:21:56,820
And if anyone wants to
ask for clarifications,

561
00:21:56,820 --> 00:21:58,060
this would be a good time.

562
00:21:58,060 --> 00:22:00,697
And then we'll actually go
and reverse engineer and say,

563
00:22:00,697 --> 00:22:03,030
everything that's going on
in a one-layer attention-only

564
00:22:03,030 --> 00:22:06,270
transformer is now in
the palm of our hands.

565
00:22:06,270 --> 00:22:07,772
It's a very toy model.

566
00:22:07,772 --> 00:22:10,230
No one actually uses one-layer
attention-only transformers.

567
00:22:10,230 --> 00:22:13,140
But we'll be able to understand
the one-layer attention-only

568
00:22:13,140 --> 00:22:14,121
transformer.

569
00:22:14,121 --> 00:22:16,308

570
00:22:16,308 --> 00:22:17,850
So just to be clear,
so you're saying

571
00:22:17,850 --> 00:22:22,888
that the query-key circuit here
is learning retention rates?

572
00:22:22,888 --> 00:22:24,555
And essentially, it
just ends up running

573
00:22:24,555 --> 00:22:27,570
some of the attention
between different tokens?

574
00:22:27,570 --> 00:22:29,270
Yeah, yeah.

575
00:22:29,270 --> 00:22:33,360
So this matrix, when it--
yeah, all three of those parts

576
00:22:33,360 --> 00:22:33,930
are learned.

577
00:22:33,930 --> 00:22:38,110
But that's what expresses
whether a attention pattern--

578
00:22:38,110 --> 00:22:38,610
yeah.

579
00:22:38,610 --> 00:22:40,170
That's what generates
the attention patterns,

580
00:22:40,170 --> 00:22:41,850
gets run for every
pair of tokens.

581
00:22:41,850 --> 00:22:44,280
And you can think of
values in that matrix

582
00:22:44,280 --> 00:22:46,170
as just being how
much every token wants

583
00:22:46,170 --> 00:22:49,070
to attend to every other token
if it was in the context.

584
00:22:49,070 --> 00:22:50,820
We're ignoring positional
embeddings here.

585
00:22:50,820 --> 00:22:52,560
So there's a little bit that
we're sort of aligning over

586
00:22:52,560 --> 00:22:53,590
there as well.

587
00:22:53,590 --> 00:22:55,290
But sort of in a
global sense, how much

588
00:22:55,290 --> 00:22:57,498
does every token want to
attend to every other token?

589
00:22:57,498 --> 00:22:58,050
Right.

590
00:22:58,050 --> 00:23:00,300
And the other circuit, like,
the output-value circuit,

591
00:23:00,300 --> 00:23:05,970
is using the attention that's
calculated to, I guess,

592
00:23:05,970 --> 00:23:07,950
affect the final outputs?

593
00:23:07,950 --> 00:23:09,990
It's sort of saying,
if the attention head--

594
00:23:09,990 --> 00:23:11,880
assume that the attention
head attends to some token.

595
00:23:11,880 --> 00:23:14,070
So let's set aside the question
of how that gets computed.

596
00:23:14,070 --> 00:23:15,930
Just assume that it
attends to some token.

597
00:23:15,930 --> 00:23:18,472
How would it affect the outputs
if it attended to that token?

598
00:23:18,472 --> 00:23:19,110
Mm-hmm.

599
00:23:19,110 --> 00:23:21,360
And you can just calculate that.

600
00:23:21,360 --> 00:23:24,440
It's just a big table of values
that says, for this token,

601
00:23:24,440 --> 00:23:26,190
it's going to make
this token more likely.

602
00:23:26,190 --> 00:23:29,240
This token will make
this token less likely.

603
00:23:29,240 --> 00:23:29,870
Right.

604
00:23:29,870 --> 00:23:31,297
OK, and that's just--

605
00:23:31,297 --> 00:23:32,630
And it's completely independent.

606
00:23:32,630 --> 00:23:34,620
Like, it's just two
separate matrices.

607
00:23:34,620 --> 00:23:38,360
They're not-- the formulas
might make them seem entangled.

608
00:23:38,360 --> 00:23:40,630
But they're actually separate.

609
00:23:40,630 --> 00:23:41,470
Right.

610
00:23:41,470 --> 00:23:43,087
To me, it seems like the--

611
00:23:43,087 --> 00:23:45,670
like just supervision is coming
from the output value circuit.

612
00:23:45,670 --> 00:23:48,070
And the query-key circuit
seems to be more unsupervised,

613
00:23:48,070 --> 00:23:50,950
kind of thing,
because there's no--

614
00:23:50,950 --> 00:23:52,870
I mean, there are--
just, I think,

615
00:23:52,870 --> 00:23:54,950
in the sense that every--

616
00:23:54,950 --> 00:23:59,350
yeah, in a model, every
neuron is, in some sense--

617
00:23:59,350 --> 00:24:03,890
its signal is somehow downstream
from the ultimate signal.

618
00:24:03,890 --> 00:24:06,202
And so, yeah, the
output-value signal--

619
00:24:06,202 --> 00:24:07,660
the output-value
circuit is getting

620
00:24:07,660 --> 00:24:10,240
more direct-- it's perhaps
getting more direct signal.

621
00:24:10,240 --> 00:24:11,610
Correct.

622
00:24:11,610 --> 00:24:12,530
But yeah.

623
00:24:12,530 --> 00:24:13,030
Yes.

624
00:24:13,030 --> 00:24:16,770

625
00:24:16,770 --> 00:24:19,080
We will be able to dig into
this in lots of detail,

626
00:24:19,080 --> 00:24:21,690
in as much detail as you
want, in a little bit.

627
00:24:21,690 --> 00:24:23,977
So we can-- maybe
I'll push forward.

628
00:24:23,977 --> 00:24:25,560
And I think also,
actually, an example

629
00:24:25,560 --> 00:24:27,990
of how to use this to reverse
engineer a one-layer model

630
00:24:27,990 --> 00:24:31,450
will maybe make it a
little bit more motivated.

631
00:24:31,450 --> 00:24:31,950
OK.

632
00:24:31,950 --> 00:24:36,572
So just to emphasize this,
there's three different tokens

633
00:24:36,572 --> 00:24:37,530
that we can talk about.

634
00:24:37,530 --> 00:24:39,360
There's the token
that gets attended to.

635
00:24:39,360 --> 00:24:41,672
There's the token that
does the attention, which

636
00:24:41,672 --> 00:24:42,630
I call the destination.

637
00:24:42,630 --> 00:24:44,550
And then there's the
token that gets affected--

638
00:24:44,550 --> 00:24:46,967
gets the next token, which its
probabilities are affected.

639
00:24:46,967 --> 00:24:49,300

640
00:24:49,300 --> 00:24:51,520
And so something we
can do is-- notice

641
00:24:51,520 --> 00:24:54,370
that the only token that
connects to both of these

642
00:24:54,370 --> 00:24:56,320
is the token that
gets attended to.

643
00:24:56,320 --> 00:24:58,150
So these two are sort of--

644
00:24:58,150 --> 00:25:00,963
they're bridged by
their interaction

645
00:25:00,963 --> 00:25:01,880
with the source token.

646
00:25:01,880 --> 00:25:03,430
So something that's
kind of natural

647
00:25:03,430 --> 00:25:05,918
is to ask, for a
given source token,

648
00:25:05,918 --> 00:25:07,585
how does it interact
with both of these?

649
00:25:07,585 --> 00:25:10,210

650
00:25:10,210 --> 00:25:13,890
So let's take, for instance,
the token "perfect."

651
00:25:13,890 --> 00:25:15,990
Which tokens-- one
thing we can ask is,

652
00:25:15,990 --> 00:25:19,410
which tokens want to
attend to "perfect"?

653
00:25:19,410 --> 00:25:21,240
Well, apparently, the
tokens that most want

654
00:25:21,240 --> 00:25:24,682
to attend to perfect are
"are," and "looks," and "is,"

655
00:25:24,682 --> 00:25:26,640
and "provides."

656
00:25:26,640 --> 00:25:29,215
So "are" is the most, "looks"
is the next most, and so on.

657
00:25:29,215 --> 00:25:30,840
And then, when we
attend to "perfect"--

658
00:25:30,840 --> 00:25:33,182
and this is with one
single attention head,

659
00:25:33,182 --> 00:25:35,640
so it'd be different if we did
a different attention head--

660
00:25:35,640 --> 00:25:38,015
it wants to really increase
the probability of "perfect,"

661
00:25:38,015 --> 00:25:40,380
and then, to a lesser extent,
"super," and "absolute,"

662
00:25:40,380 --> 00:25:42,120
and "pure."

663
00:25:42,120 --> 00:25:45,390
And we can ask, what
sequences of tokens

664
00:25:45,390 --> 00:25:50,768
are made more likely by
this particular set of--

665
00:25:50,768 --> 00:25:53,310
this particular set of things
wanting to attend to each other

666
00:25:53,310 --> 00:25:54,670
and becoming more likely?

667
00:25:54,670 --> 00:25:57,300
Well, things of the form--

668
00:25:57,300 --> 00:25:59,250
we have our token that
we attended back to.

669
00:25:59,250 --> 00:26:01,768
And we have some skip of
some number of tokens.

670
00:26:01,768 --> 00:26:03,060
They don't have to be adjacent.

671
00:26:03,060 --> 00:26:05,100
But then, later on, we
see the token "are."

672
00:26:05,100 --> 00:26:07,080
And it attends back to
"perfect" and increases

673
00:26:07,080 --> 00:26:09,450
the probability of "perfect."

674
00:26:09,450 --> 00:26:11,130
So you can think
of these as being--

675
00:26:11,130 --> 00:26:12,870
like, we're sort of
creating-- changing

676
00:26:12,870 --> 00:26:15,450
the probability of what we
might call skip trigrams, where

677
00:26:15,450 --> 00:26:18,587
we have-- we skip over a
bunch of tokens in the middle.

678
00:26:18,587 --> 00:26:20,420
But we're affecting the
probability, really,

679
00:26:20,420 --> 00:26:23,460
of trigrams, so
"perfect," "are perfect,"

680
00:26:23,460 --> 00:26:25,435
"perfect," "looks super."

681
00:26:25,435 --> 00:26:26,560
We can look at another one.

682
00:26:26,560 --> 00:26:28,770
So we get the token "large."

683
00:26:28,770 --> 00:26:30,510
These tokens,
"contains," "using,"

684
00:26:30,510 --> 00:26:32,400
"specify," want to go
and look back to it,

685
00:26:32,400 --> 00:26:33,390
and an increase
in the probability

686
00:26:33,390 --> 00:26:34,590
of "large" and "small."

687
00:26:34,590 --> 00:26:36,540
And the skip trigrams
that are affected

688
00:26:36,540 --> 00:26:39,180
are things like
"large," "using large,"

689
00:26:39,180 --> 00:26:45,100
"large," "contains small,"
and things like this.

690
00:26:45,100 --> 00:26:47,760
If we see the number two,
we increase the probability

691
00:26:47,760 --> 00:26:48,810
of other numbers.

692
00:26:48,810 --> 00:26:50,790
And we affect the
probability tokens,

693
00:26:50,790 --> 00:26:57,590
or skip trigrams, like "two,"
"one two," "two," "has three."

694
00:26:57,590 --> 00:27:00,447
Now, you're all in
a technical field.

695
00:27:00,447 --> 00:27:02,030
So you'll probably
recognize this one.

696
00:27:02,030 --> 00:27:03,680
We have "lambda."

697
00:27:03,680 --> 00:27:05,807
And then we see backslash.

698
00:27:05,807 --> 00:27:07,640
And then we want to
increase the probability

699
00:27:07,640 --> 00:27:10,280
of "lambda," and "sorted,"
and "lambda," and "operator."

700
00:27:10,280 --> 00:27:12,620
So it's all LaTeX.

701
00:27:12,620 --> 00:27:14,917
It wants to-- if
it sees "lambda,"

702
00:27:14,917 --> 00:27:17,000
it thinks that maybe next
time, I use a backslash.

703
00:27:17,000 --> 00:27:22,100
I should go and put in
some LaTeX math symbol.

704
00:27:22,100 --> 00:27:23,990
Also, same thing for HTML.

705
00:27:23,990 --> 00:27:26,900
We see "nbsp" for a
non-breaking space.

706
00:27:26,900 --> 00:27:27,980
Then we see an ampersand.

707
00:27:27,980 --> 00:27:29,923
We want to go and
make that more likely.

708
00:27:29,923 --> 00:27:31,340
The takeaway from
all this is that

709
00:27:31,340 --> 00:27:33,382
a one-layer attention-only
transformer is totally

710
00:27:33,382 --> 00:27:37,608
acting on these skip trigrams.

711
00:27:37,608 --> 00:27:38,650
Everything that it does--

712
00:27:38,650 --> 00:27:39,720
I mean, I guess it
also has this pathway

713
00:27:39,720 --> 00:27:40,887
by which it affects bigrams.

714
00:27:40,887 --> 00:27:43,448
But mostly, it's just
affecting these skip trigrams.

715
00:27:43,448 --> 00:27:44,490
And there's lots of them.

716
00:27:44,490 --> 00:27:46,763
It's just, like, these giant
tables of skip trigrams

717
00:27:46,763 --> 00:27:48,180
that are made more
or less likely.

718
00:27:48,180 --> 00:27:51,002

719
00:27:51,002 --> 00:27:52,710
There's lots of other
fun things it does.

720
00:27:52,710 --> 00:27:53,970
Sometimes the
tokenization will split up

721
00:27:53,970 --> 00:27:55,360
a word in multiple ways.

722
00:27:55,360 --> 00:27:57,820
So, like, we have "indy."

723
00:27:57,820 --> 00:27:59,290
Well, that's not a good example.

724
00:27:59,290 --> 00:28:00,510
We have the word "Pike."

725
00:28:00,510 --> 00:28:05,730
And then we see the token P.
And then we predict "ike."

726
00:28:05,730 --> 00:28:08,820
And we predict "spikes"
and stuff like that.

727
00:28:08,820 --> 00:28:10,650
Or these ones are kind of fun.

728
00:28:10,650 --> 00:28:12,983
Maybe they're actually worth
talking about for a second.

729
00:28:12,983 --> 00:28:15,780
So we see the token "Lloyd."

730
00:28:15,780 --> 00:28:20,190
And then we see an L, and
maybe we predict "Lloyd," or R,

731
00:28:20,190 --> 00:28:24,167
and we predict "Ralph,"
C, "Catherine."

732
00:28:24,167 --> 00:28:26,250
But we'll see in a second
that-- well, yeah, we'll

733
00:28:26,250 --> 00:28:27,300
come back to that in a sec.

734
00:28:27,300 --> 00:28:29,700
So we increase the probability
of things like "Lloyd," "Lloyd"

735
00:28:29,700 --> 00:28:30,900
and "Lloyd," "Catherine."

736
00:28:30,900 --> 00:28:35,640
Or "Pixmap"-- if anyone's
worked with QT, we see "Pixmap,"

737
00:28:35,640 --> 00:28:39,540
and we increase the
probability of P,

738
00:28:39,540 --> 00:28:42,750
"Pixmap" again, but
also Q, "Canvas."

739
00:28:42,750 --> 00:28:47,008

740
00:28:47,008 --> 00:28:48,800
But of course, there's
a problem with this,

741
00:28:48,800 --> 00:28:51,640
which is, it doesn't get
to pick which one of these

742
00:28:51,640 --> 00:28:52,870
goes with which one.

743
00:28:52,870 --> 00:28:56,800
So if you want to go and
make "Pixmap," "Pixmap"

744
00:28:56,800 --> 00:28:59,560
and "Pixmap," "QCanvas"
more profitable,

745
00:28:59,560 --> 00:29:02,200
you also have to go
and make "Pixmap,"

746
00:29:02,200 --> 00:29:04,750
"PCanvas" more probable.

747
00:29:04,750 --> 00:29:07,000
And if you want to make
"Lloyd," "Lloyd" and "Lloyd,"

748
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:09,130
"Catherine" more
probable, you also

749
00:29:09,130 --> 00:29:13,030
have to make "Lloyd," "Cloyd"
and "Lloyd," "Latherine"

750
00:29:13,030 --> 00:29:14,680
more probable.

751
00:29:14,680 --> 00:29:16,810
And so there's actually
bugs that transformers

752
00:29:16,810 --> 00:29:19,565
have, like weird-- at
least in these really tiny,

753
00:29:19,565 --> 00:29:21,190
one-layer attachment-only
transformers,

754
00:29:21,190 --> 00:29:23,050
there's these bugs
that-- they seem

755
00:29:23,050 --> 00:29:24,610
weird until you
realize that it's

756
00:29:24,610 --> 00:29:28,860
this giant table of skip
trigrams that's operating.

757
00:29:28,860 --> 00:29:31,600
And the nature of that is
that you're going to be--

758
00:29:31,600 --> 00:29:34,570

759
00:29:34,570 --> 00:29:36,070
it sort of forces
you, if you want

760
00:29:36,070 --> 00:29:37,690
to go and do this,
to go and also make

761
00:29:37,690 --> 00:29:40,610
some weird predictions.

762
00:29:40,610 --> 00:29:43,160
Chris, quickly,
is there a reason

763
00:29:43,160 --> 00:29:45,350
why the source tokens
here you have a space

764
00:29:45,350 --> 00:29:46,880
before the first character?

765
00:29:46,880 --> 00:29:48,320
Yes.

766
00:29:48,320 --> 00:29:50,420
That's just the-- I
was giving examples

767
00:29:50,420 --> 00:29:53,130
where the tokenization
breaks in a particular way.

768
00:29:53,130 --> 00:29:53,630
And--

769
00:29:53,630 --> 00:29:54,500
OK.

770
00:29:54,500 --> 00:29:58,827
--because spaces get included
in the tokenization when

771
00:29:58,827 --> 00:30:00,410
there's a space in
front of something,

772
00:30:00,410 --> 00:30:02,210
and then there's an example
where the space isn't

773
00:30:02,210 --> 00:30:04,820
in front of it, they can get
tokenized in different ways.

774
00:30:04,820 --> 00:30:05,390
Got it.

775
00:30:05,390 --> 00:30:05,890
Cool.

776
00:30:05,890 --> 00:30:06,540
Thanks.

777
00:30:06,540 --> 00:30:07,040
Yeah.

778
00:30:07,040 --> 00:30:07,665
Great question.

779
00:30:07,665 --> 00:30:12,060

780
00:30:12,060 --> 00:30:14,340
OK, so just to abstract
away some common patterns

781
00:30:14,340 --> 00:30:17,820
that we're seeing, I think one
pretty common thing is what you

782
00:30:17,820 --> 00:30:20,770
might describe as like b, a, b.

783
00:30:20,770 --> 00:30:22,698
So you go, and you
see some token,

784
00:30:22,698 --> 00:30:25,240
and then you see another token
that might precede that token.

785
00:30:25,240 --> 00:30:26,698
And then you're
like, ah, probably,

786
00:30:26,698 --> 00:30:29,490
the token that I saw earlier
is going to occur again.

787
00:30:29,490 --> 00:30:32,400
Or sometimes you predict a
slightly different token.

788
00:30:32,400 --> 00:30:36,990
So maybe an example of the
first one is two, one, two.

789
00:30:36,990 --> 00:30:39,758
But you can also
do two, has, three.

790
00:30:39,758 --> 00:30:42,300
And so three isn't the same as
two, but it's kind of similar.

791
00:30:42,300 --> 00:30:43,390
So that's one thing.

792
00:30:43,390 --> 00:30:44,860
Another one is
this example, where

793
00:30:44,860 --> 00:30:45,850
you have a token
that-- something

794
00:30:45,850 --> 00:30:47,308
that's tokenized
together one time,

795
00:30:47,308 --> 00:30:48,670
and then it's split apart.

796
00:30:48,670 --> 00:30:49,668
So you see the token.

797
00:30:49,668 --> 00:30:51,210
And then you see
something that might

798
00:30:51,210 --> 00:30:52,350
be the first part of the token.

799
00:30:52,350 --> 00:30:53,892
And then you predict
the second part.

800
00:30:53,892 --> 00:30:56,965

801
00:30:56,965 --> 00:30:59,090
I think the thing that's
really striking about this

802
00:30:59,090 --> 00:31:02,780
is these are all, in some
ways, a really crude kind

803
00:31:02,780 --> 00:31:05,380
of in-context learning.

804
00:31:05,380 --> 00:31:09,050
And in particular, these
models get about 0.1 nats

805
00:31:09,050 --> 00:31:11,318
rather than about 0.4 nats
of in-context learning.

806
00:31:11,318 --> 00:31:13,110
And they never go
through the phase change.

807
00:31:13,110 --> 00:31:15,720
So they're doing some kind
of really crude in-context

808
00:31:15,720 --> 00:31:16,220
learning.

809
00:31:16,220 --> 00:31:18,440
And also, they're dedicating
almost all their attention

810
00:31:18,440 --> 00:31:20,490
heads to this kind of
crude in-context learning.

811
00:31:20,490 --> 00:31:23,210
So they're not very good at
it, but they're dedicating

812
00:31:23,210 --> 00:31:26,480
their capacity to it.

813
00:31:26,480 --> 00:31:29,160
I'm noticing that it's 10:37.

814
00:31:29,160 --> 00:31:31,310
I want to just check
how long I can go,

815
00:31:31,310 --> 00:31:34,830
because maybe I should like
super-accelerate if this is--

816
00:31:34,830 --> 00:31:37,440
Chris, I think it's fine,
because students are also

817
00:31:37,440 --> 00:31:39,040
asking questions in between.

818
00:31:39,040 --> 00:31:41,030
So you should be good.

819
00:31:41,030 --> 00:31:42,860
OK, so maybe my plan
will be that I'll

820
00:31:42,860 --> 00:31:44,460
talk until like 10:55 or 11:00.

821
00:31:44,460 --> 00:31:44,960
Yeah, sure.

822
00:31:44,960 --> 00:31:48,050
And then if you guys
want, I can go and answer

823
00:31:48,050 --> 00:31:50,540
questions for a
while after that.

824
00:31:50,540 --> 00:31:51,980
Yeah, it works.

825
00:31:51,980 --> 00:31:53,130
Fantastic.

826
00:31:53,130 --> 00:31:55,220
So you can see this
as a very crude kind

827
00:31:55,220 --> 00:31:56,210
of in-context learning.

828
00:31:56,210 --> 00:31:57,832
Like, basically,
what we're saying is,

829
00:31:57,832 --> 00:32:00,290
it's sort of all the flavor
of, OK, well, I saw this token.

830
00:32:00,290 --> 00:32:02,450
Probably, these other
tokens, the same token

831
00:32:02,450 --> 00:32:04,838
or similar tokens are more
likely to go and occur later.

832
00:32:04,838 --> 00:32:06,380
And look, this is
an opportunity that

833
00:32:06,380 --> 00:32:07,670
sort of looks like
I could inject

834
00:32:07,670 --> 00:32:08,878
the token that I saw earlier.

835
00:32:08,878 --> 00:32:11,300
I'm going to inject it here
and say that it's more likely.

836
00:32:11,300 --> 00:32:13,490
That's basically
what it's doing.

837
00:32:13,490 --> 00:32:16,100
And it's dedicating almost
all of its capacity to that.

838
00:32:16,100 --> 00:32:17,760
So it's sort of the
opposite of what we

839
00:32:17,760 --> 00:32:19,010
thought with RNNs in the past.

840
00:32:19,010 --> 00:32:20,120
Like, it used to be
that everyone was like,

841
00:32:20,120 --> 00:32:22,490
oh, RNNs, it's so hard
to get them to care

842
00:32:22,490 --> 00:32:24,215
about long-distance contexts.

843
00:32:24,215 --> 00:32:27,440
You know, maybe we need to
go and use dams or something.

844
00:32:27,440 --> 00:32:27,970
No.

845
00:32:27,970 --> 00:32:29,720
If you train a
transformer, it dedicates--

846
00:32:29,720 --> 00:32:31,430
and you give it a
long enough context,

847
00:32:31,430 --> 00:32:33,920
it's dedicating almost
all of its capacity

848
00:32:33,920 --> 00:32:38,720
to this type of stuff, which
is kind of interesting.

849
00:32:38,720 --> 00:32:40,220
There are some
attention heads which

850
00:32:40,220 --> 00:32:42,350
are more primarily positional.

851
00:32:42,350 --> 00:32:44,713
Usually, we-- in
the model that I've

852
00:32:44,713 --> 00:32:46,130
been training that
has two layer--

853
00:32:46,130 --> 00:32:48,350
or it's only a one-layer
model-- has 12 attention heads.

854
00:32:48,350 --> 00:32:50,000
And usually, around
two or three of those

855
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:51,380
will become these
more positional, sort

856
00:32:51,380 --> 00:32:53,360
of shorter-term things
that do something more

857
00:32:53,360 --> 00:32:55,367
like local trigram statistics.

858
00:32:55,367 --> 00:32:57,575
And then everything else
becomes these skip trigrams.

859
00:32:57,575 --> 00:33:01,970

860
00:33:01,970 --> 00:33:05,040
Yeah, so some
takeaways from this.

861
00:33:05,040 --> 00:33:07,380
You can understand one-layer
attention-only transformers

862
00:33:07,380 --> 00:33:10,710
in terms of these
OV and QK circuits.

863
00:33:10,710 --> 00:33:13,110
Transformers desperately want
to do in-context learning.

864
00:33:13,110 --> 00:33:15,750
They desperately,
desperately, desperately want

865
00:33:15,750 --> 00:33:18,427
to go and look at these
long-distance contexts

866
00:33:18,427 --> 00:33:19,510
and go and predict things.

867
00:33:19,510 --> 00:33:21,150
There's just so much
entropy that they

868
00:33:21,150 --> 00:33:23,605
can go and reduce out of that.

869
00:33:23,605 --> 00:33:25,980
The constraints of a one-layer
attention-only transformer

870
00:33:25,980 --> 00:33:29,370
force it to make certain bugs if
it wants to do the right thing.

871
00:33:29,370 --> 00:33:31,320
And if you freeze the
attention patterns,

872
00:33:31,320 --> 00:33:34,020
these models are linear.

873
00:33:34,020 --> 00:33:36,090
OK.

874
00:33:36,090 --> 00:33:39,060
A quick aside, because
so far, this type of work

875
00:33:39,060 --> 00:33:41,310
has required us to do a lot
of very manual inspection.

876
00:33:41,310 --> 00:33:42,960
Like, we're looking through
these giant matrices.

877
00:33:42,960 --> 00:33:44,715
But there's a way that
we can escape that.

878
00:33:44,715 --> 00:33:46,590
We don't have to look
at these giant matrices

879
00:33:46,590 --> 00:33:48,180
if we don't want to.

880
00:33:48,180 --> 00:33:50,290
We can use eigenvalues
and eigenvectors.

881
00:33:50,290 --> 00:33:53,670
So recall that an eigenvalue
and an eigenvector

882
00:33:53,670 --> 00:33:57,630
just means that if you multiply
that vector by the matrix,

883
00:33:57,630 --> 00:34:00,380
it's equivalent to just scaling.

884
00:34:00,380 --> 00:34:02,673
And often, in my
experience, those

885
00:34:02,673 --> 00:34:04,590
haven't been very useful
for interpretability,

886
00:34:04,590 --> 00:34:06,890
because we're usually mapping
between different spaces.

887
00:34:06,890 --> 00:34:08,510
But if you're mapping
onto the same space,

888
00:34:08,510 --> 00:34:10,510
eigenvalues and eigenvectors
are a beautiful way

889
00:34:10,510 --> 00:34:12,150
to think about things.

890
00:34:12,150 --> 00:34:17,909
So we're going to draw
them on a radial plot.

891
00:34:17,909 --> 00:34:20,850
And we're going to have a log
radial scale, because they're

892
00:34:20,850 --> 00:34:22,850
going to vary-- their
magnitude is going to vary

893
00:34:22,850 --> 00:34:25,880
by many orders of magnitude.

894
00:34:25,880 --> 00:34:28,580
OK, so we can just go
in and our OV circuit

895
00:34:28,580 --> 00:34:29,900
maps from tokens to tokens.

896
00:34:29,900 --> 00:34:32,489
That's the same vector space
on the input and the output.

897
00:34:32,489 --> 00:34:34,072
And we can ask, you
know, what does it

898
00:34:34,072 --> 00:34:36,350
mean if we see eigenvalues
of a particular kind?

899
00:34:36,350 --> 00:34:38,060
Well, positive
eigenvalues-- and this

900
00:34:38,060 --> 00:34:40,440
is really the most important
part-- mean copying.

901
00:34:40,440 --> 00:34:41,989
So if you have a
positive eigenvalue,

902
00:34:41,989 --> 00:34:44,989
it means that there's
some set of tokens

903
00:34:44,989 --> 00:34:47,639
where if you see them, you
increase their probability.

904
00:34:47,639 --> 00:34:50,130
And if you have a lot
of positive eigenvalues,

905
00:34:50,130 --> 00:34:51,380
you're doing a lot of copying.

906
00:34:51,380 --> 00:34:53,713
If you only have positive
eigenvalues, everything you do

907
00:34:53,713 --> 00:34:55,250
is copying.

908
00:34:55,250 --> 00:34:57,458
Now, imaginary eigenvalues
mean that you see a token,

909
00:34:57,458 --> 00:34:58,958
and then you want
to go and increase

910
00:34:58,958 --> 00:35:00,470
the probability of
unrelated tokens.

911
00:35:00,470 --> 00:35:02,750
And finally, negative
eigenvalues are anti-copying.

912
00:35:02,750 --> 00:35:04,250
They're like, if
you see this token,

913
00:35:04,250 --> 00:35:07,262
you make it less
probable in the future.

914
00:35:07,262 --> 00:35:08,970
Well, that's really
nice, because now, we

915
00:35:08,970 --> 00:35:10,740
don't have to go and dig through
these giant matrices that

916
00:35:10,740 --> 00:35:12,150
are vocab size by vocab size.

917
00:35:12,150 --> 00:35:15,070
We can just look
at the eigenvalues.

918
00:35:15,070 --> 00:35:17,640
And so these are the
eigenvalues for our one-layer

919
00:35:17,640 --> 00:35:18,840
attention-only transformer.

920
00:35:18,840 --> 00:35:23,310
And we can see that
for many of these,

921
00:35:23,310 --> 00:35:25,110
they're almost
entirely positive.

922
00:35:25,110 --> 00:35:27,510
These one are sort
of entirely positive.

923
00:35:27,510 --> 00:35:29,485
These ones are almost
entirely positive.

924
00:35:29,485 --> 00:35:31,860
And really, these ones are
even almost entirely positive.

925
00:35:31,860 --> 00:35:35,010
And there's only two that
have a significant number

926
00:35:35,010 --> 00:35:37,728
of imaginary and
negative eigenvalues.

927
00:35:37,728 --> 00:35:40,270
And so what this is telling us
is-- it's just in one picture.

928
00:35:40,270 --> 00:35:44,248
We can see, OK, they're really--

929
00:35:44,248 --> 00:35:47,210
10 out of 12 of these attention
heads are just doing copying.

930
00:35:47,210 --> 00:35:49,910
They just are doing this
long-distance, well, I

931
00:35:49,910 --> 00:35:52,640
saw a token, probably it's going
to occur again, type stuff.

932
00:35:52,640 --> 00:35:53,720
That's kind of cool.

933
00:35:53,720 --> 00:35:56,940
We can summarize
it really quickly.

934
00:35:56,940 --> 00:35:58,410
OK.

935
00:35:58,410 --> 00:36:00,288
Now, the other thing
that you can-- yeah,

936
00:36:00,288 --> 00:36:01,830
so this is for a
second-- we're going

937
00:36:01,830 --> 00:36:03,538
to look at a two-layer
model in a second.

938
00:36:03,538 --> 00:36:05,340
And we'll see that
also, a lot of its heads

939
00:36:05,340 --> 00:36:07,200
are doing this kind
of copying of stuff.

940
00:36:07,200 --> 00:36:10,740
They have large
positive eigenvalues.

941
00:36:10,740 --> 00:36:11,700
You can do a histogram.

942
00:36:11,700 --> 00:36:13,140
Like, one thing
that's cool is you

943
00:36:13,140 --> 00:36:15,938
can just add up the
eigenvalues and divide them

944
00:36:15,938 --> 00:36:16,980
by their absolute values.

945
00:36:16,980 --> 00:36:18,150
And you get a number
between 0 and 1,

946
00:36:18,150 --> 00:36:19,275
which is like how copying--

947
00:36:19,275 --> 00:36:21,733
how copying is just the head--
or between negative 1 and 1,

948
00:36:21,733 --> 00:36:22,980
how copying is just the head.

949
00:36:22,980 --> 00:36:23,870
And you can just do a histogram.

950
00:36:23,870 --> 00:36:25,912
And you can see, oh yeah,
almost all of the heads

951
00:36:25,912 --> 00:36:28,570
are doing lots of copying.

952
00:36:28,570 --> 00:36:30,990
It's nice to be able to go
and summarize our model in a--

953
00:36:30,990 --> 00:36:32,550
and I think this
is sort of like,

954
00:36:32,550 --> 00:36:34,500
we've gone through a
very bottom-up way.

955
00:36:34,500 --> 00:36:36,000
And we didn't start
with assumptions

956
00:36:36,000 --> 00:36:36,760
about what the model is doing.

957
00:36:36,760 --> 00:36:38,160
We tried to understand
its structure.

958
00:36:38,160 --> 00:36:40,368
And then we were able to
summarize it in useful ways.

959
00:36:40,368 --> 00:36:43,740
And now we're able to go
and say something about it.

960
00:36:43,740 --> 00:36:45,280
Now, another thing
you might ask is,

961
00:36:45,280 --> 00:36:47,410
what do you think the
eigenvalues of the QK circuit

962
00:36:47,410 --> 00:36:47,910
mean?

963
00:36:47,910 --> 00:36:49,577
And in our examples
so far, they haven't

964
00:36:49,577 --> 00:36:51,785
been that-- they wouldn't
have been that interesting.

965
00:36:51,785 --> 00:36:53,040
But in a minute, they will be.

966
00:36:53,040 --> 00:36:54,750
And so I'll briefly
describe what they mean.

967
00:36:54,750 --> 00:36:56,458
A positive eigenvalue
would mean you want

968
00:36:56,458 --> 00:36:58,200
to attend to the same tokens.

969
00:36:58,200 --> 00:36:59,845
An imaginary
eigenvalue-- and this

970
00:36:59,845 --> 00:37:01,595
is what you would
mostly see in the models

971
00:37:01,595 --> 00:37:03,012
that we've seen
so far-- means you

972
00:37:03,012 --> 00:37:06,540
want to go in and attend to an
unrelated or a different token.

973
00:37:06,540 --> 00:37:08,160
And a negative
eigenvalue would mean

974
00:37:08,160 --> 00:37:12,260
you want to avoid attending
to the same token.

975
00:37:12,260 --> 00:37:14,903
So that will be
relevant in a second.

976
00:37:14,903 --> 00:37:16,320
Yeah, so those are
going to mostly

977
00:37:16,320 --> 00:37:18,528
be useful to think about in
multilayer attention-only

978
00:37:18,528 --> 00:37:20,903
transformers, when we can have
chains of attention heads.

979
00:37:20,903 --> 00:37:22,390
And so we can ask,
you know-- well,

980
00:37:22,390 --> 00:37:23,950
I'll get to that in a second.

981
00:37:23,950 --> 00:37:26,470
Yeah, so that's a
table summarizing that.

982
00:37:26,470 --> 00:37:28,660
Unfortunately, this approach
completely breaks down

983
00:37:28,660 --> 00:37:29,860
once you have MLP layers.

984
00:37:29,860 --> 00:37:32,313
MLP layers, now you have
these nonlinearities.

985
00:37:32,313 --> 00:37:33,730
And so you don't
get this property

986
00:37:33,730 --> 00:37:35,147
where your model
is mostly linear,

987
00:37:35,147 --> 00:37:36,564
and you can just
look at a matrix.

988
00:37:36,564 --> 00:37:39,064
But if you're working with only
attention-only transformers,

989
00:37:39,064 --> 00:37:40,990
this is a very nice way
to think about things.

990
00:37:40,990 --> 00:37:41,490
OK.

991
00:37:41,490 --> 00:37:43,960
So recall that one-layer
attention-only transformers

992
00:37:43,960 --> 00:37:45,640
don't undergo the space
change that we talked about

993
00:37:45,640 --> 00:37:46,000
in the beginning.

994
00:37:46,000 --> 00:37:47,375
Like, right now,
we're on a hunt.

995
00:37:47,375 --> 00:37:49,417
We're trying to go and
answer this mystery of how

996
00:37:49,417 --> 00:37:51,625
the-- what the hell is going
on in that phase change,

997
00:37:51,625 --> 00:37:53,980
where models suddenly get
good at in-context learning.

998
00:37:53,980 --> 00:37:55,000
We want to answer that.

999
00:37:55,000 --> 00:37:56,817
And one-layer
attention-only transformers

1000
00:37:56,817 --> 00:37:58,150
don't undergo that phase change.

1001
00:37:58,150 --> 00:38:00,280
But two-layer attention-only
transformers do.

1002
00:38:00,280 --> 00:38:02,488
So we'd like to know what's
different about two-layer

1003
00:38:02,488 --> 00:38:03,888
attention-only transformers.

1004
00:38:03,888 --> 00:38:07,560

1005
00:38:07,560 --> 00:38:09,212
OK, well-- so in
our previous-- when

1006
00:38:09,212 --> 00:38:11,670
we were dealing with one-layer
attention-only transformers,

1007
00:38:11,670 --> 00:38:14,520
we were able to go and
rewrite them in this form.

1008
00:38:14,520 --> 00:38:16,470
And it gave us a lot of
structure-- of ability

1009
00:38:16,470 --> 00:38:18,970
to go and understand the model,
because we could go and say,

1010
00:38:18,970 --> 00:38:20,100
well, this is bigrams.

1011
00:38:20,100 --> 00:38:22,470
And then each one of these
is looking somewhere.

1012
00:38:22,470 --> 00:38:24,053
And we have this
matrix that describes

1013
00:38:24,053 --> 00:38:26,230
how it affects things.

1014
00:38:26,230 --> 00:38:28,980
And yeah, so that gave
us a lot of ability

1015
00:38:28,980 --> 00:38:30,853
to think about these things.

1016
00:38:30,853 --> 00:38:33,270
And we can also just write it
in this factored form, where

1017
00:38:33,270 --> 00:38:35,770
we have the embedding, and then
we have the attention heads,

1018
00:38:35,770 --> 00:38:38,030
and then we have
the unembedding.

1019
00:38:38,030 --> 00:38:38,600
OK.

1020
00:38:38,600 --> 00:38:41,930
Well-- oh, and for
simplicity, we often

1021
00:38:41,930 --> 00:38:45,560
go and write WOV for WO
WV, because they always

1022
00:38:45,560 --> 00:38:46,160
come together.

1023
00:38:46,160 --> 00:38:47,035
It's always the case.

1024
00:38:47,035 --> 00:38:48,620
Like, it's in some
sense an illusion

1025
00:38:48,620 --> 00:38:50,395
that WO and WV are
different matrices.

1026
00:38:50,395 --> 00:38:51,770
They're just one
low-rank matrix.

1027
00:38:51,770 --> 00:38:53,645
They're never-- they're
always used together.

1028
00:38:53,645 --> 00:38:56,120
And similarly, WQ and WK,
it's sort of an illusion

1029
00:38:56,120 --> 00:38:58,293
that they're different matrices.

1030
00:38:58,293 --> 00:38:59,960
There they're always
just used together.

1031
00:38:59,960 --> 00:39:01,918
And keys and queries are
just sort of-- they're

1032
00:39:01,918 --> 00:39:04,750
just an artifact of
these low-rank matrices.

1033
00:39:04,750 --> 00:39:08,070
So in any case, it's useful to
go and write those together.

1034
00:39:08,070 --> 00:39:08,570
OK, great.

1035
00:39:08,570 --> 00:39:11,480
So a two-layer attention-only
transformer, what we do

1036
00:39:11,480 --> 00:39:14,750
is we go through the
embedding matrix.

1037
00:39:14,750 --> 00:39:17,060
Then we go through the
layer 1 attention heads.

1038
00:39:17,060 --> 00:39:19,018
Then we go through the
layer 2 attention heads.

1039
00:39:19,018 --> 00:39:20,643
And then we go through
the unembedding.

1040
00:39:20,643 --> 00:39:22,400
And for the attention
heads, we always

1041
00:39:22,400 --> 00:39:23,965
have this identity
as well, which

1042
00:39:23,965 --> 00:39:26,090
corresponds to just going
down the residual stream.

1043
00:39:26,090 --> 00:39:28,430
So we can go down
the residual stream,

1044
00:39:28,430 --> 00:39:30,243
or we can go through
an attention head.

1045
00:39:30,243 --> 00:39:32,660
On the next step, we can also
go down the residual stream,

1046
00:39:32,660 --> 00:39:34,285
or we can go through
an attention head.

1047
00:39:34,285 --> 00:39:38,105

1048
00:39:38,105 --> 00:39:40,910
And there's this useful
identity, the mixed product

1049
00:39:40,910 --> 00:39:44,600
identity, that any tensor
product or other ways

1050
00:39:44,600 --> 00:39:46,880
of interpreting
this obey, which is

1051
00:39:46,880 --> 00:39:49,992
that if you have an attention
head, and we have, say--

1052
00:39:49,992 --> 00:39:51,950
we have the weights, and
the attention pattern,

1053
00:39:51,950 --> 00:39:54,410
and the WOV matrix, and
the attention pattern.

1054
00:39:54,410 --> 00:39:56,210
The attention patterns
multiply together.

1055
00:39:56,210 --> 00:39:59,390
And the OV circuits
multiply together.

1056
00:39:59,390 --> 00:40:00,950
And they behave nicely.

1057
00:40:00,950 --> 00:40:01,610
OK, great.

1058
00:40:01,610 --> 00:40:04,225
So we can just expand
out that equation.

1059
00:40:04,225 --> 00:40:05,600
We can just take
that big product

1060
00:40:05,600 --> 00:40:06,140
we had at the beginning.

1061
00:40:06,140 --> 00:40:07,370
And we can just expand it out.

1062
00:40:07,370 --> 00:40:09,120
And we get three
different kinds of terms.

1063
00:40:09,120 --> 00:40:11,162
So one thing we do is
we get this path that

1064
00:40:11,162 --> 00:40:13,370
just goes directly through
the residual stream, where

1065
00:40:13,370 --> 00:40:14,270
we embed and unembed.

1066
00:40:14,270 --> 00:40:16,590
And that's going to want
to represent some bigram

1067
00:40:16,590 --> 00:40:19,500
statistics.

1068
00:40:19,500 --> 00:40:22,620
Then we get things that look
like the attention head terms

1069
00:40:22,620 --> 00:40:23,970
that we had previously.

1070
00:40:23,970 --> 00:40:26,490

1071
00:40:26,490 --> 00:40:30,660
And finally, we get
these terms that

1072
00:40:30,660 --> 00:40:32,671
correspond to going through
two attention heads.

1073
00:40:32,671 --> 00:40:37,480

1074
00:40:37,480 --> 00:40:39,970
Now, it's worth noting that
these terms are not actually

1075
00:40:39,970 --> 00:40:42,580
the same as their-- because
the attention head--

1076
00:40:42,580 --> 00:40:44,380
the attention patterns
in the second layer

1077
00:40:44,380 --> 00:40:46,720
can be computed from the
outputs of the first layer,

1078
00:40:46,720 --> 00:40:48,590
those are also going
to be more expressive.

1079
00:40:48,590 --> 00:40:50,180
But at a high level,
you can think of there

1080
00:40:50,180 --> 00:40:52,097
as being these three
different kinds of terms.

1081
00:40:52,097 --> 00:40:54,700
And we sometimes called these
terms virtual attention heads,

1082
00:40:54,700 --> 00:40:56,620
because they don't
exist in the sense--

1083
00:40:56,620 --> 00:40:57,910
like, they aren't sort
of explicitly represented

1084
00:40:57,910 --> 00:40:58,600
in the model.

1085
00:40:58,600 --> 00:41:00,698
But they, in fact--

1086
00:41:00,698 --> 00:41:01,990
they have an attention pattern.

1087
00:41:01,990 --> 00:41:03,030
They have no read circuit.

1088
00:41:03,030 --> 00:41:04,988
They're sort of, in almost
all functional ways,

1089
00:41:04,988 --> 00:41:06,580
like a tiny little
attention head.

1090
00:41:06,580 --> 00:41:08,740
And there's exponentially
many of them.

1091
00:41:08,740 --> 00:41:10,240
It turns out they're
not going to be

1092
00:41:10,240 --> 00:41:11,448
that important in this model.

1093
00:41:11,448 --> 00:41:14,270
But in other models,
they can be important.

1094
00:41:14,270 --> 00:41:14,770
Right.

1095
00:41:14,770 --> 00:41:16,200
So one thing that's
nice about this

1096
00:41:16,200 --> 00:41:18,040
is it allows us to think about
attention heads in a really

1097
00:41:18,040 --> 00:41:18,665
principled way.

1098
00:41:18,665 --> 00:41:22,090
We don't have to go
and think about--

1099
00:41:22,090 --> 00:41:25,090
I think there's-- like, people
look at attention patterns all

1100
00:41:25,090 --> 00:41:25,820
the time.

1101
00:41:25,820 --> 00:41:28,813
And I think a concern you
would have as well-- there's

1102
00:41:28,813 --> 00:41:29,980
multiple attention patterns.

1103
00:41:29,980 --> 00:41:32,070
Like, the information that's
being moved by one attention

1104
00:41:32,070 --> 00:41:34,210
head, it might have been move
there by another attention head

1105
00:41:34,210 --> 00:41:35,460
and not have originated there.

1106
00:41:35,460 --> 00:41:37,655
And it might still be
moved somewhere else.

1107
00:41:37,655 --> 00:41:40,280
But in fact, this gives us a way
to avoid all of those concerns

1108
00:41:40,280 --> 00:41:43,780
and just think about things
in a single principled way.

1109
00:41:43,780 --> 00:41:46,570
OK, in any case, an
important question to ask

1110
00:41:46,570 --> 00:41:48,790
is, how important how are
these different terms?

1111
00:41:48,790 --> 00:41:50,165
Like, we could
study all of them.

1112
00:41:50,165 --> 00:41:52,240
How important are they?

1113
00:41:52,240 --> 00:41:54,850
And it turns out, you can just--

1114
00:41:54,850 --> 00:41:57,645
there's an algorithm you can use
where you knock out attention--

1115
00:41:57,645 --> 00:41:59,020
knock out these
terms, and you go

1116
00:41:59,020 --> 00:42:01,300
and you ask, how
important are they?

1117
00:42:01,300 --> 00:42:04,540
And it turns out that by
far, the most important thing

1118
00:42:04,540 --> 00:42:06,370
is these individual
attention head

1119
00:42:06,370 --> 00:42:09,070
terms-- in this model, by
far the most important thing.

1120
00:42:09,070 --> 00:42:11,050
The virtual attention
heads basically

1121
00:42:11,050 --> 00:42:12,760
don't matter that much.

1122
00:42:12,760 --> 00:42:15,670
They only have an effect of 0.3
nats, using to the above ones.

1123
00:42:15,670 --> 00:42:17,360
And the bigrams are
still pretty useful.

1124
00:42:17,360 --> 00:42:19,277
So if we want to try to
understand this model,

1125
00:42:19,277 --> 00:42:21,622
we should probably go and
focus our attention on--

1126
00:42:21,622 --> 00:42:23,080
the virtual attention
heads are not

1127
00:42:23,080 --> 00:42:27,065
going to be the best way to
go and focus our attention,

1128
00:42:27,065 --> 00:42:28,690
especially since
there's a lot of them.

1129
00:42:28,690 --> 00:42:31,030
There's 124 of
them for 0.3 nats.

1130
00:42:31,030 --> 00:42:33,670
It's very little that you
would understand for studying

1131
00:42:33,670 --> 00:42:35,030
one of those terms.

1132
00:42:35,030 --> 00:42:36,820
So the thing that we
probably want to do--

1133
00:42:36,820 --> 00:42:38,690
we know that these
are bigram statistics.

1134
00:42:38,690 --> 00:42:40,107
So what we really
want to do is we

1135
00:42:40,107 --> 00:42:43,010
want to understand the
individual attention head

1136
00:42:43,010 --> 00:42:43,510
terms.

1137
00:42:43,510 --> 00:42:47,123

1138
00:42:47,123 --> 00:42:48,040
This is the algorithm.

1139
00:42:48,040 --> 00:42:49,750
I'm going to skip
over it for time.

1140
00:42:49,750 --> 00:42:53,000
We can ignore that term
because it's small.

1141
00:42:53,000 --> 00:42:55,000
And it turns out also
that the layer 2 attention

1142
00:42:55,000 --> 00:42:57,730
heads are doing way more
than layer 1 attention heads.

1143
00:42:57,730 --> 00:42:59,200
And that's not that surprising.

1144
00:42:59,200 --> 00:43:00,700
Like, the layer
2 attention heads

1145
00:43:00,700 --> 00:43:01,660
are more expressive
because they can

1146
00:43:01,660 --> 00:43:04,160
use the layer 1 attention heads
to construct their attention

1147
00:43:04,160 --> 00:43:05,680
patterns.

1148
00:43:05,680 --> 00:43:07,665
OK, so if we could
just go and understand

1149
00:43:07,665 --> 00:43:09,040
the layer 2
attention heads, we'd

1150
00:43:09,040 --> 00:43:14,200
probably understand a lot of
what's going on in this model.

1151
00:43:14,200 --> 00:43:16,360
And the trick is that the
attention heads are now

1152
00:43:16,360 --> 00:43:18,160
constructed from
the previous layer,

1153
00:43:18,160 --> 00:43:19,730
rather than just
from the tokens.

1154
00:43:19,730 --> 00:43:22,150
So this is still the
same, the attention head.

1155
00:43:22,150 --> 00:43:24,320
The attention pattern
is more complex.

1156
00:43:24,320 --> 00:43:26,890
And if you write it out, you
get this complex equation that

1157
00:43:26,890 --> 00:43:28,473
says, you embed the tokens.

1158
00:43:28,473 --> 00:43:29,890
Then you go and
you shuffle things

1159
00:43:29,890 --> 00:43:31,807
around using the attention
heads for the keys.

1160
00:43:31,807 --> 00:43:33,130
Then you multiply it by WQK.

1161
00:43:33,130 --> 00:43:34,630
Then you multiply
it, shuffle things

1162
00:43:34,630 --> 00:43:35,620
around again for the queries.

1163
00:43:35,620 --> 00:43:37,510
And then you go and
multiply by the embedding

1164
00:43:37,510 --> 00:43:38,590
again, because
they were embedded.

1165
00:43:38,590 --> 00:43:40,090
And then you get
back to the tokens.

1166
00:43:40,090 --> 00:43:44,030

1167
00:43:44,030 --> 00:43:45,390
But let's actually look at them.

1168
00:43:45,390 --> 00:43:48,605
So one thing that's-- remember
that when we see positive

1169
00:43:48,605 --> 00:43:50,730
eigenvalues in the OV
circuit, we're doing copying.

1170
00:43:50,730 --> 00:43:53,600
So one thing we can say
is, well, 7 out of 12,

1171
00:43:53,600 --> 00:43:56,120
and in fact, the ones with
the largest eigenvalues,

1172
00:43:56,120 --> 00:43:57,150
are doing copying.

1173
00:43:57,150 --> 00:43:59,000
So we still have a
lot of attention heads

1174
00:43:59,000 --> 00:43:59,958
that are doing copying.

1175
00:43:59,958 --> 00:44:04,150

1176
00:44:04,150 --> 00:44:06,077
And yeah, the QK circuit--

1177
00:44:06,077 --> 00:44:07,660
so one thing you
could do is you could

1178
00:44:07,660 --> 00:44:09,520
try to understand things in
terms of this more complex QK

1179
00:44:09,520 --> 00:44:09,850
equation.

1180
00:44:09,850 --> 00:44:12,183
You could also just try to
understand what the attention

1181
00:44:12,183 --> 00:44:13,510
patterns are doing empirically.

1182
00:44:13,510 --> 00:44:16,240
So let's look at one
of these copying ones.

1183
00:44:16,240 --> 00:44:18,760
I've given it the first
paragraph of Harry Potter.

1184
00:44:18,760 --> 00:44:20,468
And we can just look
at where it attends.

1185
00:44:20,468 --> 00:44:23,300

1186
00:44:23,300 --> 00:44:25,370
And something really
interesting happened.

1187
00:44:25,370 --> 00:44:27,830
So almost all the time,
we just attend back

1188
00:44:27,830 --> 00:44:31,100
to the first token we have, the
special token at the beginning

1189
00:44:31,100 --> 00:44:32,210
of the sequence.

1190
00:44:32,210 --> 00:44:35,160
And we usually think of that
as just being a null attention

1191
00:44:35,160 --> 00:44:35,660
operation.

1192
00:44:35,660 --> 00:44:37,050
It's a way for it
to not do anything.

1193
00:44:37,050 --> 00:44:39,380
In fact, if you look the value
vector, it's basically zero.

1194
00:44:39,380 --> 00:44:41,422
It's just not copying any
information from there.

1195
00:44:41,422 --> 00:44:44,190

1196
00:44:44,190 --> 00:44:46,310
But whenever we
see repeated text,

1197
00:44:46,310 --> 00:44:47,560
something interesting happens.

1198
00:44:47,560 --> 00:44:50,100
So when we get to
"Mr," it tries to look

1199
00:44:50,100 --> 00:44:51,750
at "and." it's a
little bit weak.

1200
00:44:51,750 --> 00:44:56,510
Then we get to "D," and
it attends to "urs."

1201
00:44:56,510 --> 00:44:57,860
That's interesting.

1202
00:44:57,860 --> 00:45:04,150
And then we get to "urs,"
and it tends to "ley."

1203
00:45:04,150 --> 00:45:07,490
And so it's not attending
to the same token.

1204
00:45:07,490 --> 00:45:12,640
It's attending to the same
token, shifted one forward.

1205
00:45:12,640 --> 00:45:14,020
Well, that's really interesting.

1206
00:45:14,020 --> 00:45:15,940
And there's actually a
lot of attention heads

1207
00:45:15,940 --> 00:45:16,510
that are doing this.

1208
00:45:16,510 --> 00:45:18,730
So here, we have one where
now, we hit the Potters,

1209
00:45:18,730 --> 00:45:20,638
"Pot," then we attended "ters."

1210
00:45:20,638 --> 00:45:22,180
Maybe that's the
same attention head.

1211
00:45:22,180 --> 00:45:24,670
I don't remember when I was
constructing this example.

1212
00:45:24,670 --> 00:45:26,420
It turns out this is
a super common thing.

1213
00:45:26,420 --> 00:45:29,003
So you go and you look
for the previous example.

1214
00:45:29,003 --> 00:45:29,920
You shift one forward.

1215
00:45:29,920 --> 00:45:31,520
And you're like, OK, well,
last time I saw this,

1216
00:45:31,520 --> 00:45:32,260
this is what happened.

1217
00:45:32,260 --> 00:45:34,093
Probably, the same thing
is going to happen.

1218
00:45:34,093 --> 00:45:37,290

1219
00:45:37,290 --> 00:45:39,720
And we can go and
look at the effect

1220
00:45:39,720 --> 00:45:42,027
that the attention
head has on the logits.

1221
00:45:42,027 --> 00:45:43,860
Most of the time, it's
not affecting things.

1222
00:45:43,860 --> 00:45:46,080
But in these cases, it's
able to go and predict--

1223
00:45:46,080 --> 00:45:48,455
when it's doing this, this
thing of going and looking one

1224
00:45:48,455 --> 00:45:51,353
forward, it's able to go
and predict the next token.

1225
00:45:51,353 --> 00:45:52,770
So we call this
an induction head.

1226
00:45:52,770 --> 00:45:54,960
An induction head looks
for the previous copy,

1227
00:45:54,960 --> 00:45:56,700
looks forward, and
says, ah, probably

1228
00:45:56,700 --> 00:45:58,770
the same thing that happened
last time is going to happen.

1229
00:45:58,770 --> 00:46:00,895
You can think of this as
being a nearest neighbors.

1230
00:46:00,895 --> 00:46:03,183
It's like an in-context
nearest neighbors algorithm.

1231
00:46:03,183 --> 00:46:05,100
It's going and searching
through your context,

1232
00:46:05,100 --> 00:46:06,990
finding similar things,
and then predicting

1233
00:46:06,990 --> 00:46:08,448
that's what's going
to happen next.

1234
00:46:08,448 --> 00:46:11,550

1235
00:46:11,550 --> 00:46:14,850
The way that these
actually work is--

1236
00:46:14,850 --> 00:46:16,410
I mean, there's
actually two ways.

1237
00:46:16,410 --> 00:46:18,780
But in a model that uses
rotary attention or something

1238
00:46:18,780 --> 00:46:22,500
like this, you only have one.

1239
00:46:22,500 --> 00:46:24,270
You shift your key--

1240
00:46:24,270 --> 00:46:26,263
first, you-- an
earlier attention head

1241
00:46:26,263 --> 00:46:27,430
shifts your key forward one.

1242
00:46:27,430 --> 00:46:30,060
So you would, like, take the
value of the previous token,

1243
00:46:30,060 --> 00:46:32,280
and you embed it in
your present token.

1244
00:46:32,280 --> 00:46:37,110
And then you have your query
and your key go and look at--

1245
00:46:37,110 --> 00:46:38,220
yeah, try to go and match.

1246
00:46:38,220 --> 00:46:40,500
So you look for the same thing.

1247
00:46:40,500 --> 00:46:43,390
And then you go and you
predict that whatever you saw

1248
00:46:43,390 --> 00:46:44,960
is going to be the next token.

1249
00:46:44,960 --> 00:46:47,163
So that's the
high-level algorithm.

1250
00:46:47,163 --> 00:46:48,580
Sometimes you can
do clever things

1251
00:46:48,580 --> 00:46:51,070
where, actually, it'll care
about multiple earlier tokens.

1252
00:46:51,070 --> 00:46:52,900
And it'll look for, like,
short phrases and so on.

1253
00:46:52,900 --> 00:46:54,440
So induction heads
can really vary

1254
00:46:54,440 --> 00:46:56,727
in how much of the previous
context they care about

1255
00:46:56,727 --> 00:46:59,060
or what aspects of the previous
context they care about.

1256
00:46:59,060 --> 00:47:01,690
But this general trick of
looking for the same thing,

1257
00:47:01,690 --> 00:47:06,910
shift forward, predict that,
is what induction heads do.

1258
00:47:06,910 --> 00:47:08,440
Lots of examples of this.

1259
00:47:08,440 --> 00:47:10,870
And the cool thing
is you can now--

1260
00:47:10,870 --> 00:47:13,510
you can use the QK eigenvalues
to characterize this.

1261
00:47:13,510 --> 00:47:16,175
You can say, well, we're looking
for the same thing, shifted

1262
00:47:16,175 --> 00:47:17,800
by one, but looking
for the same thing.

1263
00:47:17,800 --> 00:47:19,300
If you expand
through the attention

1264
00:47:19,300 --> 00:47:21,220
heads in the right
way, that'll work out.

1265
00:47:21,220 --> 00:47:22,120
And we're copying.

1266
00:47:22,120 --> 00:47:24,040
And so an induction
head is one which

1267
00:47:24,040 --> 00:47:27,160
has both positive OV
eigenvalues, and also

1268
00:47:27,160 --> 00:47:29,246
positive QK eigenvalues.

1269
00:47:29,246 --> 00:47:32,405

1270
00:47:32,405 --> 00:47:34,030
And so you can just
put that on a plot.

1271
00:47:34,030 --> 00:47:36,880
And you have your induction
heads in the corner.

1272
00:47:36,880 --> 00:47:40,480
So you have your OV eigenvalues,
your QK eigenvalues.

1273
00:47:40,480 --> 00:47:42,190
And I think, actually,
OV is this axis.

1274
00:47:42,190 --> 00:47:42,880
QK is this one.

1275
00:47:42,880 --> 00:47:44,500
Axis doesn't matter.

1276
00:47:44,500 --> 00:47:47,550
And in the corner, you
have your eigenvalues,

1277
00:47:47,550 --> 00:47:49,600
or your induction heads.

1278
00:47:49,600 --> 00:47:52,240

1279
00:47:52,240 --> 00:47:54,040
Yeah, and so this seems to be--

1280
00:47:54,040 --> 00:47:54,565
well, OK.

1281
00:47:54,565 --> 00:47:55,940
We now have an
actual hypothesis.

1282
00:47:55,940 --> 00:47:58,420
The hypothesis is, the way
that-- that phase change

1283
00:47:58,420 --> 00:48:00,640
we're seeing, the phase
change is the discovery

1284
00:48:00,640 --> 00:48:01,682
of these induction heads.

1285
00:48:01,682 --> 00:48:04,330
That would be the hypothesis.

1286
00:48:04,330 --> 00:48:06,850
And these are way more
effective than regular--

1287
00:48:06,850 --> 00:48:08,390
than this first
algorithm we had,

1288
00:48:08,390 --> 00:48:09,730
which was just sort
of blindly copy things

1289
00:48:09,730 --> 00:48:11,030
wherever it could be plausible.

1290
00:48:11,030 --> 00:48:12,967
Now we can go and actually
recognize patterns,

1291
00:48:12,967 --> 00:48:15,550
and look at what happened, and
predict that similar things are

1292
00:48:15,550 --> 00:48:16,467
going to happen again.

1293
00:48:16,467 --> 00:48:17,936
That's a way better algorithm.

1294
00:48:17,936 --> 00:48:20,608

1295
00:48:20,608 --> 00:48:22,400
Yeah, so there's other
attention heads that

1296
00:48:22,400 --> 00:48:23,270
are doing more local things.

1297
00:48:23,270 --> 00:48:25,940
I'm going to go and skip over
that and return to our mystery,

1298
00:48:25,940 --> 00:48:26,960
because I am
running out of time.

1299
00:48:26,960 --> 00:48:28,002
I have five more minutes.

1300
00:48:28,002 --> 00:48:31,070
OK, so what is going on with
this in-context learning?

1301
00:48:31,070 --> 00:48:32,480
Well, now we have a hypothesis.

1302
00:48:32,480 --> 00:48:33,453
Let's check it.

1303
00:48:33,453 --> 00:48:35,120
So we think it might
be induction heads.

1304
00:48:35,120 --> 00:48:37,720

1305
00:48:37,720 --> 00:48:39,470
And there's a few
reasons we believe this.

1306
00:48:39,470 --> 00:48:43,340
So one thing is going to
be that induction heads--

1307
00:48:43,340 --> 00:48:44,060
well, OK.

1308
00:48:44,060 --> 00:48:45,477
I'll just go over
to the evidence.

1309
00:48:45,477 --> 00:48:47,150
So one thing you can
do is you can just

1310
00:48:47,150 --> 00:48:49,310
oblate the attention heads.

1311
00:48:49,310 --> 00:48:51,260
And it turns out you
can color-- here,

1312
00:48:51,260 --> 00:48:53,930
we have attention heads
colored by how much they

1313
00:48:53,930 --> 00:48:55,620
are an induction head.

1314
00:48:55,620 --> 00:48:57,840
And this is the
start of the bump.

1315
00:48:57,840 --> 00:48:59,797
This is the end
of the bump here.

1316
00:48:59,797 --> 00:49:02,130
And we can see that they--
first of all, induction heads

1317
00:49:02,130 --> 00:49:02,475
are forming.

1318
00:49:02,475 --> 00:49:04,770
Like, previously, we didn't
have induction heads here.

1319
00:49:04,770 --> 00:49:06,660
Now, they're just
starting to form here.

1320
00:49:06,660 --> 00:49:11,100
And then we have really intense
induction heads here and here.

1321
00:49:11,100 --> 00:49:16,425
And the attention heads where
you oblate them, you get a--

1322
00:49:16,425 --> 00:49:19,225
you got a loss in-- so we're
looking not at the loss,

1323
00:49:19,225 --> 00:49:21,600
but at this meta-learning
score, the difference between--

1324
00:49:21,600 --> 00:49:23,730
or an in-context learning
score, the difference

1325
00:49:23,730 --> 00:49:27,240
between the 500th token
and the 50th token.

1326
00:49:27,240 --> 00:49:30,493
That's all explained
by induction heads.

1327
00:49:30,493 --> 00:49:32,160
Now, we actually have
one induction head

1328
00:49:32,160 --> 00:49:33,030
that doesn't contribute to it.

1329
00:49:33,030 --> 00:49:34,510
Actually, it does the opposite.

1330
00:49:34,510 --> 00:49:36,310
So that's kind of interesting.

1331
00:49:36,310 --> 00:49:38,237
Maybe it's doing something
shorter distance.

1332
00:49:38,237 --> 00:49:40,320
And there's also this
interesting thing where they

1333
00:49:40,320 --> 00:49:41,700
all rush to be induction heads.

1334
00:49:41,700 --> 00:49:44,363
And then they discover only
a few win out in the end.

1335
00:49:44,363 --> 00:49:46,530
So there's some interesting
dynamics going on there.

1336
00:49:46,530 --> 00:49:49,740
But it really seems like
in these small models,

1337
00:49:49,740 --> 00:49:52,275
all of in-context learning is
explained by these induction

1338
00:49:52,275 --> 00:49:54,430
heads.

1339
00:49:54,430 --> 00:49:56,203
OK, but what about large models?

1340
00:49:56,203 --> 00:49:58,120
Well, in large models,
it's going to be harder

1341
00:49:58,120 --> 00:49:58,912
to go and ask this.

1342
00:49:58,912 --> 00:50:01,580
But one thing you can
do is you can ask, OK,

1343
00:50:01,580 --> 00:50:05,290
we can look at our induction--
or our in-context learning

1344
00:50:05,290 --> 00:50:06,190
score over time.

1345
00:50:06,190 --> 00:50:07,660
We get this sharp phase change.

1346
00:50:07,660 --> 00:50:09,040
Oh, look.

1347
00:50:09,040 --> 00:50:12,947
Induction heads form at
exactly the same point in time.

1348
00:50:12,947 --> 00:50:14,530
So that's only
correlational evidence.

1349
00:50:14,530 --> 00:50:16,810
But it's pretty suggestive
correlational evidence,

1350
00:50:16,810 --> 00:50:19,270
especially given that we
have an obvious-- like,

1351
00:50:19,270 --> 00:50:21,270
the obvious the fact that
induction heads should

1352
00:50:21,270 --> 00:50:21,958
have is this.

1353
00:50:21,958 --> 00:50:24,250
I guess it could be that
there's other mechanisms being

1354
00:50:24,250 --> 00:50:26,083
discovered at the same
time in large models.

1355
00:50:26,083 --> 00:50:28,090
But it has to be in
a very small window.

1356
00:50:28,090 --> 00:50:30,430
So really suggests
that the thing

1357
00:50:30,430 --> 00:50:34,610
that's driving that change
is in-context learning.

1358
00:50:34,610 --> 00:50:40,240
OK, so obviously, induction
heads can go and copy text.

1359
00:50:40,240 --> 00:50:42,827
But a question you might ask
is, can they do translation?

1360
00:50:42,827 --> 00:50:44,410
Like, there's all
these amazing things

1361
00:50:44,410 --> 00:50:48,877
that models can do that it's
not obvious in-context learning,

1362
00:50:48,877 --> 00:50:50,710
or this sort of copying
mechanism, could do.

1363
00:50:50,710 --> 00:50:56,540
So I just want to very quickly
look at a few fun examples.

1364
00:50:56,540 --> 00:51:01,440
So here, we have an
attention pattern.

1365
00:51:01,440 --> 00:51:03,944
Oh, I guess I need
to open Lexoscope.

1366
00:51:03,944 --> 00:51:08,300

1367
00:51:08,300 --> 00:51:09,140
Hmm.

1368
00:51:09,140 --> 00:51:10,513
Let me try doing that again.

1369
00:51:10,513 --> 00:51:12,680
Sorry, I should have thought
this through a bit more

1370
00:51:12,680 --> 00:51:15,490
before this talk.

1371
00:51:15,490 --> 00:51:17,240
Chris, could you zoom
in a little, please?

1372
00:51:17,240 --> 00:51:18,090
Yeah, yeah.

1373
00:51:18,090 --> 00:51:18,590
Thank you.

1374
00:51:18,590 --> 00:51:39,060

1375
00:51:39,060 --> 00:51:41,400
OK, I'm not-- my French
isn't that great.

1376
00:51:41,400 --> 00:51:43,770
But my name is Christopher.

1377
00:51:43,770 --> 00:51:45,653
I'm from Canada.

1378
00:51:45,653 --> 00:51:47,070
What we can do
here is we can look

1379
00:51:47,070 --> 00:51:49,410
at where this
attention head attends

1380
00:51:49,410 --> 00:51:50,820
as we go and we do this.

1381
00:51:50,820 --> 00:51:54,580
And it'll become especially
clear on the second sentence.

1382
00:51:54,580 --> 00:51:56,610
So here, we're on the period.

1383
00:51:56,610 --> 00:51:58,620
And we tend to "je."

1384
00:51:58,620 --> 00:52:01,680
Now we're on-- and
"je" is I in French.

1385
00:52:01,680 --> 00:52:02,220
OK.

1386
00:52:02,220 --> 00:52:05,970
Now we're on the "I,"
and we attend to "suis."

1387
00:52:05,970 --> 00:52:07,800
And now we're on
the "am," and we

1388
00:52:07,800 --> 00:52:12,190
attend to "du," which is from,
and then "from" to "Canada."

1389
00:52:12,190 --> 00:52:15,120
And so we're doing a
cross-lingual induction

1390
00:52:15,120 --> 00:52:18,190
head, which we can
use for translation.

1391
00:52:18,190 --> 00:52:20,380
And indeed, if you
look at examples,

1392
00:52:20,380 --> 00:52:23,230
this is where it seems to--
this to be a major driving

1393
00:52:23,230 --> 00:52:25,570
force in the model's
ability to go

1394
00:52:25,570 --> 00:52:28,870
and correctly do translation.

1395
00:52:28,870 --> 00:52:31,990
Another fun example is--

1396
00:52:31,990 --> 00:52:33,640
I think maybe the
most impressive thing

1397
00:52:33,640 --> 00:52:36,520
about in-context learning to
me has been the model's ability

1398
00:52:36,520 --> 00:52:38,075
to go and learn
arbitrary functions.

1399
00:52:38,075 --> 00:52:39,700
Like, you just show
the model function.

1400
00:52:39,700 --> 00:52:41,242
It can start mimicking
that function.

1401
00:52:41,242 --> 00:52:42,100
Well, OK.

1402
00:52:42,100 --> 00:52:44,120
Chris, I have a question.

1403
00:52:44,120 --> 00:52:44,770
Yes?

1404
00:52:44,770 --> 00:52:48,070
So do these induction heads only
do kind of a look-ahead copy,

1405
00:52:48,070 --> 00:52:54,370
or can they also do some sort of
complex structure recognition?

1406
00:52:54,370 --> 00:52:55,150
Yeah, yeah.

1407
00:52:55,150 --> 00:52:59,200
So they can both use a larger
context, previous context,

1408
00:52:59,200 --> 00:53:01,030
and they can copy
more abstract things.

1409
00:53:01,030 --> 00:53:03,370
So like, the translation one is
showing you that they can copy,

1410
00:53:03,370 --> 00:53:05,590
rather than the literal
token, a translated version.

1411
00:53:05,590 --> 00:53:08,620
So it's what I call
soft induction head.

1412
00:53:08,620 --> 00:53:11,250
And yeah, you can have
them copy similar words.

1413
00:53:11,250 --> 00:53:13,000
You can have them look
at longer contexts.

1414
00:53:13,000 --> 00:53:15,850
It can look for more
structural things.

1415
00:53:15,850 --> 00:53:18,310
The way that we usually
characterize them is whether--

1416
00:53:18,310 --> 00:53:20,290
in large models-- is
whether they empirically

1417
00:53:20,290 --> 00:53:21,540
behave like an induction head.

1418
00:53:21,540 --> 00:53:23,893
So the definition gets
a little bit blurry

1419
00:53:23,893 --> 00:53:25,810
when you try to encompass
these more-- there's

1420
00:53:25,810 --> 00:53:27,405
sort of a blurry boundary.

1421
00:53:27,405 --> 00:53:29,530
But yeah, there seem to be
a lot of attention heads

1422
00:53:29,530 --> 00:53:32,320
that are doing sort of more
and more abstract versions.

1423
00:53:32,320 --> 00:53:32,820
OK.

1424
00:53:32,820 --> 00:53:33,730
Thank you.

1425
00:53:33,730 --> 00:53:34,450
Yeah.

1426
00:53:34,450 --> 00:53:36,920
My favorite version is this
one that I'm about to show you,

1427
00:53:36,920 --> 00:53:38,620
which is used--

1428
00:53:38,620 --> 00:53:40,900
let's isolate a single
one of these-- which

1429
00:53:40,900 --> 00:53:42,920
can do pattern recognition.

1430
00:53:42,920 --> 00:53:45,680
So it can learn functions in the
context and learn how to do it.

1431
00:53:45,680 --> 00:53:49,300
So I've just made up a
nonsense function here.

1432
00:53:49,300 --> 00:53:51,430
We're going to encode
one binary variable

1433
00:53:51,430 --> 00:53:54,370
with a choice of whether
to do a color or a month

1434
00:53:54,370 --> 00:53:56,280
as the first word.

1435
00:53:56,280 --> 00:54:00,370
Then we're going to-- so
we have green or June here.

1436
00:54:00,370 --> 00:54:03,110
Let's zoom in more.

1437
00:54:03,110 --> 00:54:07,010
So we have color or month
and animal or fruit.

1438
00:54:07,010 --> 00:54:09,398
And then we have to map it
to either true or false.

1439
00:54:09,398 --> 00:54:10,190
So that's our goal.

1440
00:54:10,190 --> 00:54:11,280
And it's going to be an XOR.

1441
00:54:11,280 --> 00:54:13,488
So we have a binary variable
represented in this way.

1442
00:54:13,488 --> 00:54:14,900
We do an XOR.

1443
00:54:14,900 --> 00:54:17,477
I'm pretty confident this was
never in the training set,

1444
00:54:17,477 --> 00:54:18,560
because I just made it up.

1445
00:54:18,560 --> 00:54:21,290
And it seems like
a nonsense problem.

1446
00:54:21,290 --> 00:54:23,900
OK, so then we can go
and ask, can the model

1447
00:54:23,900 --> 00:54:24,650
go and parse that?

1448
00:54:24,650 --> 00:54:25,250
Well, it can.

1449
00:54:25,250 --> 00:54:27,230
And it uses induction
heads to do it.

1450
00:54:27,230 --> 00:54:29,035
And what we can do is
we can look at the--

1451
00:54:29,035 --> 00:54:30,410
so we look at a
colon, where it's

1452
00:54:30,410 --> 00:54:32,420
going to go and try and
predict the next word.

1453
00:54:32,420 --> 00:54:35,960
And for instance, here,
we have April dog.

1454
00:54:35,960 --> 00:54:38,180
So it's a month
and then an animal.

1455
00:54:38,180 --> 00:54:39,590
And it should be true.

1456
00:54:39,590 --> 00:54:41,850
And what it does is it
looks for a previous--

1457
00:54:41,850 --> 00:54:44,020
previous cases where
there was an animal--

1458
00:54:44,020 --> 00:54:45,770
a month and then an
animal, especially one

1459
00:54:45,770 --> 00:54:47,930
where the month was the
same, and goes, and looks,

1460
00:54:47,930 --> 00:54:49,640
and says that it's true.

1461
00:54:49,640 --> 00:54:52,760
And so the model can go and
learn a function, a completely

1462
00:54:52,760 --> 00:54:55,130
arbitrary function,
by going and doing

1463
00:54:55,130 --> 00:54:59,290
this kind of pattern
recognition induction head.

1464
00:54:59,290 --> 00:55:01,290
And so this, to me, made
it a lot more plausible

1465
00:55:01,290 --> 00:55:07,260
that these models actually
can do in-context learning.

1466
00:55:07,260 --> 00:55:09,300
Like, the generality of
all these amazing things

1467
00:55:09,300 --> 00:55:12,540
we see these large
language models do

1468
00:55:12,540 --> 00:55:14,118
can be explained
by induction heads.

1469
00:55:14,118 --> 00:55:14,910
We don't know that.

1470
00:55:14,910 --> 00:55:16,868
It could be that there's
other things going on.

1471
00:55:16,868 --> 00:55:19,560
It's very possible that there's
lots of other things going on.

1472
00:55:19,560 --> 00:55:21,390
But it seems a lot
more plausible to me

1473
00:55:21,390 --> 00:55:24,310
than it did when we started.

1474
00:55:24,310 --> 00:55:26,502
I'm conscious that I
am actually over time.

1475
00:55:26,502 --> 00:55:28,710
Let me just quickly go
through these last few slides.

1476
00:55:28,710 --> 00:55:29,830
Yeah, so I think,
thinking about this

1477
00:55:29,830 --> 00:55:31,872
as like an in-context
nearest neighbors, I think,

1478
00:55:31,872 --> 00:55:33,955
is a really useful way
to think about this.

1479
00:55:33,955 --> 00:55:36,880
Other things could
absolutely be contributing.

1480
00:55:36,880 --> 00:55:39,460
This might explain
why transformers

1481
00:55:39,460 --> 00:55:44,160
do in-context learning over a
long context better than LSTMs.

1482
00:55:44,160 --> 00:55:46,182
An LSTM can't do
this, because it's not

1483
00:55:46,182 --> 00:55:47,890
linear in the amount
of compute it needs.

1484
00:55:47,890 --> 00:55:51,220
It's like quadratic or n log
n if it was really clever.

1485
00:55:51,220 --> 00:55:53,750
So transformers-- or LSTMs,
impossible to do this.

1486
00:55:53,750 --> 00:55:55,465
Transformers do do this.

1487
00:55:55,465 --> 00:55:57,340
And actually, they
diverge at the same point,

1488
00:55:57,340 --> 00:55:58,750
that if you looked--

1489
00:55:58,750 --> 00:56:03,425
well, I can go into this in
more detail after if you want.

1490
00:56:03,425 --> 00:56:05,800
There's a really nice paper
by Marcus Hutter explaining--

1491
00:56:05,800 --> 00:56:07,300
trying to predict
and explain why we

1492
00:56:07,300 --> 00:56:08,420
observe scaling laws in models.

1493
00:56:08,420 --> 00:56:10,503
It's worth noting that the
arguments in this paper

1494
00:56:10,503 --> 00:56:13,620
go exactly through
to this example--

1495
00:56:13,620 --> 00:56:14,200
this theory.

1496
00:56:14,200 --> 00:56:15,820
In fact, they sort
